ENG.add('h3', '수능 어법 실전', {
  sub: '밑줄 어법·(A)(B)(C) 어법을 실전 순서로 푼다',
  kw: ['수일치', '준동사', '관계사', '병렬', '능동·수동', '도치', '대명사'],
  sections: [
    { h: '어법 문제 풀이 순서',
      p: `수능 어법은 <b>밑줄 하나하나를 문장 구조 속에서</b> 판단하는 문제이다. 먼저 밑줄이 들어 있는 문장의 <span class="hl">주어와 본동사</span>를 찾고, 밑줄 단어가 어떤 문법 자리에 있는지 확인한다. 해석보다 구조가 먼저이며, 해석은 확인용으로 쓴다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일', '체크 포인트'], rows: [
        ['1', '문장의 주어·본동사 표시', '수식어(전치사구·관계절)를 괄호로 묶기'],
        ['2', '밑줄이 동사 자리인지, 준동사 자리인지 판단', '접속사 없이 동사 두 개 금지'],
        ['3', '밑줄 앞뒤 구조 확인', '완전한 절인가, 불완전한 절인가'],
        ['4', '의미로 최종 확인', '능동/수동, 시제 흐름'],
      ], cap: '어법 문제는 “자리 → 구조 → 의미” 순서로 본다' },
      ex: [['The process of choosing which experiences to keep is not random.', '어떤 경험을 간직할지 고르는 과정은 무작위가 아니다. (주어 process → is)'],
           ['A coat displayed next to an expensive one looks cheap.', '비싼 코트 옆에 진열된 코트는 싸 보인다. (coat가 진열됨 → 과거분사)']],
      tip: '주어와 동사 사이가 멀수록 수일치 함정일 가능성이 크다.' },
    { h: '출제 빈도가 높은 포인트',
      p: `해마다 반복되는 포인트는 정해져 있다. <b>수일치, 동사 vs 준동사, 분사의 능동·수동, 관계대명사 what/that/which, 병렬, 대명사의 수, 형용사 vs 부사</b>가 대표적이다. 각 포인트마다 판단 기준을 한 줄로 외워 두면 시간이 크게 줄어든다.`,
      table: { head: ['출제 포인트', '판단 기준', '예'], rows: [
        ['수일치', '수식어를 걷어 내고 진짜 주어 찾기', 'The number of visitors <b>has</b> grown.'],
        ['분사', '꾸밈 받는 명사가 행위자면 -ing, 대상이면 p.p.', 'markets <b>characterized</b> by …'],
        ['what / that', '뒤가 불완전 + 앞에 선행사 없음 → what', '<b>what</b> we experience'],
        ['관계사 + 콤마', '콤마 뒤에는 that 불가 → which', ', <b>which</b> are …'],
        ['병렬', 'and/or/but 앞뒤 형태 일치', 'to abandon … and <b>change</b>'],
        ['형용사/부사', '보어 자리 → 형용사, 동사 수식 → 부사', 'dominate <b>completely</b>'],
      ] },
      ex: [['Not until the 19th century did doctors accept germ theory.', '19세기가 되어서야 의사들은 세균설을 받아들였다. (부정어 도치)'],
           ['The population of Tokyo is larger than that of Seoul.', '도쿄의 인구는 서울의 인구보다 많다. (단수 명사 → that)']] },
    { h: '자주 나오는 함정',
      p: `정답처럼 보이지만 사실은 맞는 표현, 즉 <b>“맞는데 틀려 보이는” 함정</b>에 주의한다. 부정어 도치, 대동사 do, 가주어 it, 5형식 목적격 보어 등은 어색해 보여도 올바른 경우가 많다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정', '겉보기', '실제 판단'], rows: [
        ['도치', 'Rarely does the public notice …', '부정어 문두 → 조동사 도치, 맞음'],
        ['대동사', '… than they actually do', '앞의 일반동사를 대신함, 맞음'],
        ['5형식 보어', 'leave the investment wasted', '목적어가 낭비된 상태 → p.p. 맞음'],
        ['전치사 + 관계사', 'the theory on which they rely', 'rely on → on which, 뒤는 완전한 절'],
      ] },
      warn: '“콤마 + that”, “전치사 + that”은 항상 틀린다. 반대로 that 자체가 보이면 무조건 틀렸다고 단정하지 말 것.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br>The number of students who joined the club ______ grown rapidly.`, o: [`has`, `have`, `are`, `being`], a: 0,
      why: `주어는 The number(~의 수)로 단수이므로 has가 맞다. 'a number of + 복수명사'(많은 ~)와 혼동하지 않는다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br>The scientist kept the door ______ during the experiment.`, o: [`lock`, `locking`, `locked`, `to lock`], a: 2,
      why: `keep + 목적어 + 보어에서 문(door)은 잠기는 대상이므로 과거분사 locked가 맞다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br>This is the theory ______ many researchers rely.`, o: [`which`, `on which`, `what`, `that`], a: 1,
      why: `rely on이므로 전치사를 앞으로 보낸 on which가 맞다. what은 선행사(the theory)가 있어서 쓸 수 없다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `밑줄 친 부분 중 어법상 <b>틀린</b> 것은?<br>Rarely <u>①&nbsp;does</u> the public <u>②&nbsp;notice</u> how much effort <u>③&nbsp;goes</u> into the design of everyday objects, <u>④&nbsp;that</u> are often taken for granted.`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `콤마 뒤 계속적 용법에는 that을 쓸 수 없으므로 which로 고쳐야 한다. ①은 부정어 Rarely 뒤 도치로 올바르다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `(A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 알맞은 것은?<br>Neither the manager nor the employees (A)[was / were] aware of the change, (B)[which / what] caused confusion.`, o: [`was – which`, `were – which`, `were – what`, `was – what`], a: 1,
      why: `neither A nor B는 B(the employees)에 수일치하므로 were. 앞 절 전체를 받는 계속적 용법이므로 which가 맞다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br>The population of Tokyo is larger than ______ of Seoul.`, o: [`it`, `those`, `that`, `one`], a: 2,
      why: `반복되는 단수 명사 the population을 대신하므로 that이 맞다. 복수 명사일 때 those를 쓴다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 부분 중 어법상 <b>틀린</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Scientists once believed that sleep was simply a period of rest for the brain. Recent research, however, suggests that the sleeping brain is remarkably active. During deep sleep, the neural patterns that <u>①&nbsp;were formed</u> during the day are replayed, and this repetition strengthens the connections between neurons. In one study, participants who learned a list of word pairs before a full night of sleep <u>②&nbsp;recalled</u> more of them the next morning than those who stayed awake for the same length of time. Interestingly, the benefit was greatest for information that participants had been told they <u>③&nbsp;would need</u> later. This finding implies that the brain does not store everything equally; instead, it appears to select memories based on their expected future value. The process of choosing which experiences to keep <u>④&nbsp;are</u> therefore not random but guided by goals, even when we are unaware of it.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `주어는 The process(단수)이고 of choosing … keep은 수식어이므로 are → is. ①은 패턴이 '형성되는' 것이라 수동이 맞다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 부분 중 어법상 <b>틀린</b> 것은?<div class="passage">When people decide how much to pay for a product, they rarely evaluate it in isolation. Instead, they compare it with whatever reference point is available. Retailers have long understood this tendency. A coat <u>①&nbsp;displaying</u> next to a much more expensive one tends to look like a bargain, even if its price is high by ordinary standards. Economists call this effect anchoring, and it <u>②&nbsp;has been observed</u> in settings as different as real estate negotiations and charity donations. What makes anchoring <u>③&nbsp;so</u> powerful is that it operates even when the anchor is obviously irrelevant. In one experiment, people who were asked to write down the last two digits of their phone number later offered higher prices for wine if those digits were large. Such results suggest that our sense of value is less stable than we assume, and that it is easily <u>④&nbsp;shaped</u> by the context in which choices are presented.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 0,
      why: `코트는 '진열되는' 대상이므로 displaying → displayed. ④는 가치 감각이 맥락에 의해 '형성되는' 것이므로 수동 shaped가 맞다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 부분 중 어법상 <b>틀린</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Many viewers assume that a painting's meaning lies entirely within the frame. Art historians, however, argue <u>①&nbsp;that</u> the setting in which a work is seen shapes how it is understood. A religious image <u>②&nbsp;creating</u> for a candlelit chapel, for example, was meant to be viewed in flickering light from below, and its colors and gold surfaces were chosen with this in mind. When the same image is hung in a brightly lit museum, the effects <u>③&nbsp;which</u> the artist intended may disappear entirely. Visitors see flat colors where worshippers once saw figures that seemed to move and glow. This does not mean that museums are wrong to display such works, but it does mean that <u>④&nbsp;what</u> we experience in a gallery is only one version of the object. Recognizing this gap helps us look at old art with both curiosity and humility.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 1,
      why: `그림은 예배당을 위해 '만들어진' 것이므로 creating → created. ④는 선행사 없이 experience의 목적어가 빠진 절을 이끄므로 what이 맞다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `(A), (B), (C)의 각 네모 안에서 어법에 맞는 표현으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Philosophers have long debated whether moral rules are discovered or invented. Those who believe they are discovered compare ethics to mathematics: just as the angles of a triangle (A)[add / adding] up to 180 degrees whether or not anyone notices, certain actions are wrong regardless of human opinion. Their opponents reply that moral rules vary across cultures in ways (B)[that / what] mathematical truths do not. If ethics were like geometry, they ask, why would societies disagree so deeply about it? A middle position holds that while specific rules are shaped by culture, they all respond to shared human needs such as safety and cooperation. On this view, moral codes resemble languages: each is different, yet all of them (C)[serve / serves] the same basic function of making social life possible. The debate remains open, but it reminds us that asking where morality comes from is itself a way of taking it seriously.</div>`, o: [`add – that – serve`, `adding – that – serves`, `add – what – serve`, `adding – what – serves`], a: 0,
      why: `(A) just as 절의 동사 자리이므로 add. (B) 선행사 ways가 있으므로 관계사 that. (C) all of them은 복수이므로 serve.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `(A), (B), (C)의 각 네모 안에서 어법에 맞는 표현으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">In many forests, trees are connected below ground by networks of fungi. These fungi wrap around plant roots and (A)[exchange / exchanging] nutrients with them: the fungi receive sugars produced through photosynthesis, while the trees gain access to water and minerals that their roots could not reach alone. Some researchers have gone further, claiming that trees use these networks to send resources to their offspring. This idea, (B)[which / that] has attracted wide public attention, remains controversial. Critics point out that most experiments were conducted under controlled conditions very different from those of a natural forest, and that the amount of carbon moving between trees is often too small to matter. Still, few scientists deny that underground networks play a significant role in forest health. The real question is not whether trees are connected but how (C)[important / importantly] those connections are for the survival of individual trees.</div>`, o: [`exchanging – that – important`, `exchange – which – importantly`, `exchange – which – important`, `exchanging – which – importantly`], a: 2,
      why: `(A) wrap과 and로 병렬된 동사이므로 exchange. (B) 콤마 사이 삽입된 계속적 용법이므로 which. (C) be동사 are의 보어이므로 형용사 important.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `(A), (B), (C)의 각 네모 안에서 어법에 맞는 표현으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Why do people continue to invest in a failing project? Psychologists explain this behavior through the sunk cost effect. Once we have spent time, money, or effort on something, we find it painful to abandon it, as if quitting would leave the earlier investment (A)[wasting / wasted]. A company may keep funding an unsuccessful product simply because millions have already been spent on (B)[it / them]. Rationally, however, past costs should not influence future choices, since they cannot be recovered no matter what we decide. Money already spent is gone whether the project continues or not. The only relevant question is whether the expected benefits of continuing are greater than (C)[those / that] of stopping. Recognizing the sunk cost effect does not make it disappear, but it allows decision makers to pause and ask whether they are protecting their goals or merely their pride.</div>`, o: [`wasting – it – that`, `wasted – it – those`, `wasted – them – those`, `wasting – them – that`], a: 1,
      why: `(A) 투자는 '낭비된' 상태이므로 wasted. (B) 단수 명사 an unsuccessful product를 받으므로 it. (C) 복수 benefits를 대신하므로 those.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 부분 중 어법상 <b>틀린</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Not until the nineteenth century <u>①&nbsp;did</u> physicians accept that invisible organisms could cause disease. Before then, illness was widely attributed to bad air, an idea that seemed reasonable because diseases often spread in crowded, foul-smelling places. The shift came slowly. Early microscopists had observed tiny living things, but connecting them to sickness required careful experiments <u>②&nbsp;showing</u> that specific microbes were present in every case of a particular disease. Even after such evidence appeared, many doctors resisted it, partly because accepting it meant admitting that their own unwashed hands <u>③&nbsp;had spread</u> infection among patients. The history of germ theory reveals that scientific progress depends not only on new data but also on a willingness to abandon comfortable beliefs and <u>④&nbsp;changing</u> long-established habits.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `to abandon과 and로 병렬되므로 (to) change가 되어야 한다. ①은 Not until 구문의 도치, ③은 인정하는 시점보다 앞선 일이므로 과거완료로 맞다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 부분 중 어법상 <b>틀린</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Economists distinguish between goods whose value depends on how many other people use them and goods whose value <u>①&nbsp;does</u> not. A telephone is useless if no one else owns one, but it becomes more valuable as the network grows. This property, known as a network effect, explains why certain technologies come to dominate their markets so <u>②&nbsp;complete</u>. Once a platform has attracted a large number of users, newcomers have strong reasons to join it rather than a rival, even if the rival offers better features. As a result, the most popular product is not always the best one; it is often simply the one that <u>③&nbsp;grew</u> fastest at the beginning. For regulators, this creates a difficult problem: markets <u>④&nbsp;characterized</u> by network effects tend to reward early luck rather than lasting quality, and competition may not correct the outcome on its own.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 1,
      why: `동사 dominate를 수식하므로 부사 completely가 맞다. ①은 depends를 대신하는 대동사 does, ④는 시장이 '특징지어지는' 것이므로 과거분사로 맞다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 부분 중 어법상 <b>틀린</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Human beings are surprisingly poor at predicting how they will feel in the future. When imagining an upcoming event, such as moving to a new city, people tend to focus on its most obvious feature and <u>①&nbsp;overlooking</u> the many ordinary details that will also shape their daily lives. Psychologists call this tendency focalism. Because of it, people expect positive events to make them happier, and negative ones to make them more miserable, than they actually <u>②&nbsp;do</u>. Another reason for these errors is that people underestimate their ability to adapt. Within months, most individuals return to a level of happiness close to <u>③&nbsp;what</u> they had before the event. Understanding these patterns can help us make wiser decisions, since it reminds us that few changes in circumstances <u>④&nbsp;are</u> as life-altering as they first appear.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 0,
      why: `to focus와 and로 병렬되므로 overlooking → overlook. ②는 make를 대신하는 대동사, ③은 had의 목적어가 빠진 절을 이끄는 what, ④는 복수 주어 changes에 맞춘 are로 모두 맞다.` },
  ],
});

ENG.add('h3', '수능 어휘 실전', {
  sub: '문맥상 낱말 쓰임·(A)(B)(C) 어휘를 논리로 푼다',
  kw: ['문맥 어휘', '반의어', '혼동 어휘', '글의 흐름', '논리 방향', 'context'],
  sections: [
    { h: '어휘 문제 풀이 순서',
      p: `수능 어휘 문제는 단어의 뜻만 묻지 않고 <b>글의 논리 방향과 맞는지</b>를 묻는다. 먼저 글의 주제를 잡고, 밑줄 단어가 놓인 문장이 주제를 <span class="hl">강화하는지 약화하는지</span>를 따진다. 대부분의 오답은 반의어로 바뀌어 있으므로 “반대말을 넣으면 더 자연스러운가?”를 확인한다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일', '체크 포인트'], rows: [
        ['1', '첫 2~3문장으로 주제 파악', '필자가 긍정하는가, 비판하는가'],
        ['2', '밑줄 문장의 앞뒤 인과 확인', 'so, therefore, as a result 뒤 방향'],
        ['3', '반의어 대입 테스트', '반대말이 더 자연스러우면 그것이 정답'],
        ['4', '연결어로 최종 점검', 'however, yet 뒤에는 방향 전환'],
      ], cap: '반대말을 넣어 보는 테스트가 가장 빠르다' },
      ex: [['If mimics become too common, the protection will weaken.', '흉내 내는 종이 너무 흔해지면 보호 효과는 약해진다.'],
           ['Inflation tends to widen the gap between rich and poor.', '인플레이션은 빈부 격차를 넓히는 경향이 있다.']] },
    { h: '자주 출제되는 반의어 쌍',
      p: `출제자는 원래 단어를 <b>반의어</b>로 바꿔 오답을 만든다. 아래 쌍을 짝으로 기억해 두면 밑줄을 보는 순간 의심 후보가 떠오른다.`,
      table: { head: ['원래 단어', '반의어', '뜻'], rows: [
        ['abundant', 'scarce', '풍부한 ↔ 부족한'],
        ['strengthen', 'weaken', '강화하다 ↔ 약화하다'],
        ['include', 'exclude', '포함하다 ↔ 배제하다'],
        ['confirm', 'challenge / contradict', '확인하다 ↔ 이의를 제기하다'],
        ['flexible', 'rigid / fixed', '유연한 ↔ 고정된'],
        ['depletion', 'recovery', '고갈 ↔ 회복'],
        ['heighten', 'lessen', '높이다 ↔ 줄이다'],
      ] },
      ex: [['The findings challenged the belief that cooperation fails.', '그 결과는 협력이 실패한다는 믿음에 이의를 제기했다.'],
           ['Water was scarce, so travelers saved every drop.', '물이 부족해서 여행자들은 한 방울도 아꼈다.']] },
    { h: '혼동 어휘와 함정',
      p: `철자가 비슷한 혼동 어휘도 자주 나온다. 또 <b>부정어(not, rarely, few)</b>가 있는 문장은 방향이 한 번 뒤집히므로 특히 조심한다.`,
      table: { head: ['혼동 어휘', '뜻', '예'], rows: [
        ['adapt / adopt', '적응하다 / 채택하다', 'adapt to customs / adopt a plan'],
        ['affect / effect', '영향을 주다(동) / 결과·효과(명)', 'affect mood / side effect'],
        ['complement / compliment', '보완하다 / 칭찬하다', 'complement each other'],
        ['ignore / notice', '무시하다 / 알아차리다', 'usually ignored sounds'],
      ] },
      warn: `“the problem is not ___” 처럼 부정어가 있으면 머릿속에서 not을 지우고 판단한 뒤 다시 뒤집는다.` },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `scarce의 뜻으로 알맞은 것은?`, o: [`풍부한`, `부족한`, `투명한`, `신중한`], a: 1,
      why: `scarce는 '부족한, 드문'이다. '풍부한'은 abundant이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `두 단어가 반의어 관계가 <b>아닌</b> 것은?`, o: [`include – exclude`, `expand – contract`, `reinforce – weaken`, `conceal – hide`], a: 3,
      why: `conceal과 hide는 모두 '숨기다'로 유의어이다. 나머지는 모두 반대 뜻의 짝이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br>Many immigrants try to ______ to local customs.`, o: [`adopt`, `adapt`, `adept`, `addict`], a: 1,
      why: `adapt to는 '~에 적응하다'이다. adopt는 '채택하다, 입양하다'로 to와 함께 쓰지 않는다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br>Because water was ______ in the desert, travelers carried every drop carefully.`, o: [`abundant`, `visible`, `flexible`, `scarce`], a: 3,
      why: `한 방울도 아껴 운반했다는 결과로 보아 물이 '부족했다(scarce)'가 자연스럽다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `(A), (B)에 들어갈 말로 알맞은 것은?<br>The new evidence (A)[confirmed / contradicted] the old theory, so scientists had to (B)[revise / retain] it.`, o: [`confirmed – retain`, `contradicted – revise`, `confirmed – revise`, `contradicted – retain`], a: 1,
      why: `so 뒤에서 이론을 고쳐야 했다면 앞에서는 증거가 이론과 '모순되었다(contradicted)'. 두 칸의 방향이 일치해야 한다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `문맥상 낱말의 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<br>Regular exercise <u>①&nbsp;improves</u> mood. It <u>②&nbsp;reduces</u> stress hormones and <u>③&nbsp;blocks</u> chemicals that make us feel good. Therefore, people who exercise often report <u>④&nbsp;lower</u> levels of anxiety.`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 2,
      why: `기분을 좋게 한다는 흐름이므로 좋은 기분을 만드는 화학 물질을 '막는(blocks)' 것이 아니라 '분비하는(releases)' 것이어야 한다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Many animals survive not by fighting but by deceiving. Some harmless insects have evolved bright colors that closely <u>①&nbsp;resemble</u> those of poisonous species. Predators that have learned to avoid the dangerous species therefore <u>②&nbsp;avoid</u> the harmless ones as well. Biologists have observed this pattern in butterflies, snakes, and even some flies that imitate bees. This strategy, however, works only under certain conditions. If the mimics become too <u>③&nbsp;common</u> compared with the species they copy, predators will frequently catch a harmless insect after seeing the warning colors. They will then learn that the colors do not reliably signal danger, and the protection will <u>④&nbsp;strengthen</u>. In this way, the success of deception depends on its being relatively rare. A lie that is told too often, after all, eventually stops being believed, and the same is true in nature.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `포식자가 색이 위험 신호가 아님을 배우면 보호 효과는 '약해진다(weaken)'. 마지막 문장의 '드물어야 성공한다'와도 맞아야 한다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">When prices rise steadily over time, money held in cash slowly loses its value. A sum that could buy a week's groceries ten years ago may now buy only a few days' worth. For this reason, economists often <u>①&nbsp;encourage</u> people to keep their savings in assets whose returns can keep pace with inflation. Yet this advice is not equally useful to everyone. Low-income households often need their money to remain <u>②&nbsp;accessible</u> for emergencies, and they may lack the knowledge or minimum funds needed to invest. As a result, inflation tends to <u>③&nbsp;reduce</u> the gap between rich and poor: those with investments can protect their wealth, while those who rely on cash cannot. Policies that make safe, simple savings products available to everyone can help <u>④&nbsp;ease</u> this unequal burden.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 2,
      why: `투자한 사람은 자산을 지키고 현금 의존층은 지키지 못하므로 격차를 '넓힌다(widen)'가 맞다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Composers have long known that silence is as important as sound. A brief pause before the final chord of a piece can <u>①&nbsp;lessen</u> the listener's anticipation, making the resolution feel more satisfying. Similarly, rests within a melody give the ear time to <u>②&nbsp;process</u> what it has just heard. Without them, even beautiful phrases can become tiring, much like a speech delivered without a single breath. Some twentieth-century composers went further and made silence the main subject of their works. Their aim was to draw attention to the sounds that are always present but usually <u>③&nbsp;ignored</u>, such as the hum of a room or the breathing of the audience. Such experiments showed that silence is never truly empty; it is a frame that <u>④&nbsp;shapes</u> how we hear everything around it.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 0,
      why: `마지막 화음이 더 만족스럽게 느껴지려면 쉼표가 기대감을 '높여야(heighten)' 한다. lessen은 반대 방향이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `(A), (B), (C)의 각 네모 안에서 문맥에 맞는 낱말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">It seems natural to assume that more options make people happier. However, studies of consumer behavior suggest that an (A)[abundance / shortage] of choices can be a burden. In one type of experiment, shoppers who were offered a small selection of products were more likely to make a purchase than those who faced dozens of alternatives. When options multiply, comparing them becomes mentally (B)[effortless / demanding], and people worry that they might miss a better choice. Even after deciding, they tend to feel less satisfied, because they can easily imagine the attractive features of the options they (C)[rejected / selected]. These findings do not mean that freedom of choice is harmful, but they suggest that well-designed limits can sometimes help people decide with confidence. Sometimes, it seems, fewer options lead to better and happier decisions.</div>`, o: [`abundance – effortless – selected`, `shortage – demanding – rejected`, `abundance – demanding – rejected`, `shortage – effortless – selected`], a: 2,
      why: `선택지가 '많은 것(abundance)'이 부담이 되고, 비교는 정신적으로 '힘들며(demanding)', 만족이 줄어드는 것은 '버린(rejected)' 선택지의 장점을 떠올리기 때문이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `(A), (B), (C)의 각 네모 안에서 문맥에 맞는 낱말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">A theory that can explain every possible outcome may seem powerful, but some philosophers of science regard this as a weakness rather than a strength. If no observation could ever show a theory to be wrong, then the theory tells us very little about the world. A good scientific claim, in this view, must be (A)[testable / unquestionable]: it must forbid certain events, so that their occurrence would count against it. The more a theory forbids, the more it (B)[risks / avoids], and the more informative it becomes when it survives repeated tests. This idea helps explain why scientists value bold predictions. A theory that correctly predicts a surprising result earns more (C)[credibility / suspicion] than one that merely accounts for facts already known. Boldness, in this sense, is a scientific virtue rather than a reckless gamble.</div>`, o: [`testable – risks – credibility`, `unquestionable – avoids – credibility`, `testable – avoids – suspicion`, `unquestionable – risks – suspicion`], a: 0,
      why: `틀릴 가능성이 있어야 좋은 이론이므로 '검증 가능한(testable)', 많이 금지할수록 더 많은 것을 '걸며(risks)', 놀라운 예측이 맞으면 '신뢰(credibility)'를 얻는다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `(A), (B), (C)의 각 네모 안에서 문맥에 맞는 낱말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Cities are often several degrees warmer than the surrounding countryside, a phenomenon known as the urban heat island. Dark surfaces such as asphalt and roofs (A)[absorb / reflect] large amounts of sunlight during the day and release the heat slowly at night. Because these surfaces cover much of the urban landscape, their combined effect can be considerable. At the same time, the lack of vegetation (B)[limits / increases] cooling, since plants lower temperatures by releasing water vapor. The consequences are not merely uncomfortable. Higher temperatures raise energy use for air conditioning and can be dangerous for elderly residents. Fortunately, the problem is not (C)[irreversible / manageable]. Planting trees, installing green roofs, and using lighter paving materials have all been shown to reduce urban temperatures significantly. Such measures also improve air quality and make streets more pleasant for walking.</div>`, o: [`reflect – limits – manageable`, `absorb – increases – irreversible`, `reflect – increases – manageable`, `absorb – limits – irreversible`], a: 3,
      why: `어두운 표면은 햇빛을 '흡수하고(absorb)', 식물이 없으면 냉각이 '제한된다(limits)'. 뒤에 해결책이 나오므로 문제는 '되돌릴 수 없는(irreversible)' 것이 아니다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Languages change constantly, yet speakers rarely notice the process while it is happening. A new expression usually begins in a small group, often among young people, and spreads as others adopt it. Older speakers may at first <u>①&nbsp;criticize</u> the change as careless, but within a generation the innovation can become so <u>②&nbsp;established</u> that no one remembers it was once considered incorrect. Linguists therefore treat complaints about "declining" language with some <u>③&nbsp;skepticism</u>. Every generation, they point out, has believed that its successors were damaging the language, yet languages continue to serve their speakers' needs perfectly well. What looks like decay from one point of view is, from another, a sign of <u>④&nbsp;rigidity</u>: a language that stopped changing would no longer reflect the lives of those who speak it.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `변화는 언어가 살아 있다는 증거이므로 '경직성(rigidity)'이 아니라 '생명력(vitality)'이 맞다. 콜론 뒤 '변화를 멈춘 언어는 삶을 반영하지 못한다'와 연결된다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">When a resource is shared by many users and no one owns it, each individual has an incentive to take as much as possible. A fisher who limits his catch gains little if others continue to fish heavily, so restraint seems <u>①&nbsp;pointless</u> from an individual perspective. The result can be the <u>②&nbsp;recovery</u> of the resource, harming everyone. For a long time, it was assumed that only private ownership or government control could prevent this outcome. However, field studies of fishing villages and farming communities revealed that local groups often manage shared resources <u>③&nbsp;successfully</u> on their own. They establish clear rules, monitor one another, and impose gradually increasing penalties on those who break the rules. These findings <u>④&nbsp;challenged</u> the belief that cooperation inevitably fails without outside authority.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 1,
      why: `모두가 많이 가져가면 자원이 '고갈(depletion)'되어 모두에게 해가 된다. recovery(회복)는 harming everyone과 맞지 않는다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">For much of the twentieth century, scientists believed that the adult brain was largely <u>①&nbsp;flexible</u>: after childhood, its structure was thought to change little. This view has since been <u>②&nbsp;overturned</u>. Studies of musicians, taxi drivers, and people recovering from injuries show that the brain continues to reorganize itself throughout life. Regions that are used intensively can expand, while those that are <u>③&nbsp;neglected</u> may shrink or be taken over by other functions. This capacity, called neuroplasticity, has important practical <u>④&nbsp;implications</u>. It suggests that learning new skills in adulthood is not only possible but may also help protect the brain against decline. Yet plasticity is not always beneficial. The same mechanisms that allow recovery after an injury can also deepen harmful habits, since behaviors that are repeated often become physically built into the brain and therefore harder to change.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 0,
      why: `콜론 뒤에서 어린 시절 이후 구조가 거의 변하지 않는다고 했으므로 과거의 믿음은 뇌가 '고정되어 있다(fixed)'는 것이었다.` },
  ],
});

ENG.add('h3', '빈칸 추론 고난도', {
  sub: '주제문과 재진술 단서로 빈칸을 채운다',
  kw: ['빈칸 추론', '주제문', '재진술', '대조', '연결어', '선지 대입', 'paraphrase'],
  sections: [
    { h: '빈칸 추론 풀이 순서',
      p: `빈칸 문장은 대부분 <b>글의 주제문</b>이다. 따라서 빈칸을 직접 추측하기보다 글 전체가 반복해서 말하는 내용을 찾고, 그 내용을 <span class="hl">다른 말로 바꾼(재진술) 선지</span>를 고른다. 고난도 지문일수록 예시를 지나치게 꼼꼼히 해석하지 말고 예시가 증명하려는 일반 진술에 집중한다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일', '체크 포인트'], rows: [
        ['1', '빈칸 문장을 먼저 읽기', '빈칸이 주어·목적어·결론 중 어디인가'],
        ['2', '첫 문장·연결어 뒤 문장으로 주제 잡기', 'However, In fact, In other words 뒤'],
        ['3', '빈칸과 같은 뜻의 문장(재진술) 찾기', '예시가 끝나는 지점의 요약 문장'],
        ['4', '선지 대입 후 앞뒤 연결 확인', '방향(긍정/부정)과 범위가 맞는가'],
      ], cap: '빈칸 = 주제의 재진술' },
      ex: [['A map is valuable precisely because it leaves things out.', '지도는 바로 무언가를 생략하기 때문에 가치가 있다.'],
           ['Expertise is less about general mental power than about organized knowledge.', '전문성은 일반적인 정신력보다는 조직화된 지식의 문제이다.']] },
    { h: '단서가 되는 연결어',
      p: `빈칸의 내용은 <b>연결어</b>로 예측할 수 있다. 대조 연결어 뒤에는 앞과 반대 내용이, 재진술 연결어 뒤에는 앞과 같은 내용이 온다.`,
      table: { head: ['종류', '연결어', '빈칸 예측'], rows: [
        ['대조', 'However, Yet, In contrast, Nevertheless', '앞 문장과 반대 방향'],
        ['재진술', 'In other words, That is, In this sense', '앞 문장과 같은 내용'],
        ['예시', 'For example, For instance', '빈칸 = 예시의 일반화'],
        ['인과', 'Therefore, As a result, Thus', '앞 내용의 결론'],
      ] },
      ex: [['Yet this simplicity was the result of great effort.', '그러나 이 단순함은 엄청난 노력의 결과였다.'],
           ['In other words, every system of choices influences behavior.', '다시 말해, 모든 선택 구조는 행동에 영향을 준다.']] },
    { h: '오답 선지의 함정',
      p: `고난도 빈칸의 오답은 <b>지문에 나온 단어를 그대로 쓰거나</b>, 주제와 <b>반대 방향</b>이거나, <b>범위가 너무 넓거나 좁은</b> 경우가 많다. 정답은 오히려 지문 단어를 거의 쓰지 않고 추상적으로 바꿔 말한 경우가 많다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정 유형', '특징', '대처'], rows: [
        ['단어 재활용', '지문 단어가 많이 들어 있음', '의미가 주제와 맞는지 따로 확인'],
        ['반대 방향', '주제를 뒤집은 진술', '필자의 태도(긍정/부정) 확인'],
        ['범위 오류', '일부 예시만 말하거나 지나친 일반화', '글 전체를 포괄하는가'],
        ['상식형', '맞는 말이지만 지문 근거 없음', '지문 안에서 근거 찾기'],
      ] },
      warn: '“맞는 말”과 “이 글의 빈칸에 맞는 말”은 다르다. 근거가 지문 안에 있어야 한다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `앞 내용과 <b>반대되는</b> 내용을 이끄는 연결어는?`, o: [`In other words`, `Nevertheless`, `For instance`, `Likewise`], a: 1,
      why: `Nevertheless(그럼에도 불구하고)는 대조·양보를 나타낸다. In other words는 재진술, For instance는 예시, Likewise는 유사를 나타낸다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `앞 문장을 <b>다른 말로 바꾸어 말할 때</b> 쓰는 연결어는?`, o: [`As a result`, `On the contrary`, `Nonetheless`, `In other words`], a: 3,
      why: `In other words(다시 말해)는 재진술 신호이다. 빈칸이 이 뒤에 있으면 앞 내용을 그대로 바꿔 말한 선지가 정답이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `빈칸에 가장 알맞은 것은?<br>Good teachers do not simply give answers; they ask questions that lead students to find answers ______.`, o: [`from textbooks`, `too quickly`, `by themselves`, `without thinking`], a: 2,
      why: `답을 그냥 주지 않는다(not simply give answers)는 앞부분과 대조되어, 학생 '스스로(by themselves)' 찾게 한다는 흐름이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `빈칸에 가장 알맞은 것은?<br>A map that showed every tree and stone would be as large as the land itself and therefore useless. A map is valuable precisely because it ______.`, o: [`leaves things out`, `includes every detail`, `is printed in color`, `was made by experts`], a: 0,
      why: `모든 것을 담은 지도는 쓸모없다고 했으므로 지도의 가치는 '생략하는 것'에 있다. includes every detail은 반대 방향이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `빈칸에 가장 알맞은 것은?<br>Critics often praised the novel for its simplicity. Yet this simplicity was ______: the author rewrote each chapter dozens of times to remove anything unnecessary.`, o: [`a sign of laziness`, `the result of great effort`, `borrowed from other writers`, `unnoticed by readers`], a: 1,
      why: `콜론 뒤 '수십 번 고쳐 썼다'가 빈칸의 근거이므로 '큰 노력의 결과'가 맞다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `빈칸에 가장 알맞은 것은?<br>Bees that find rich flowers return to the hive and perform a dance. The angle and length of the dance tell other bees where the food is. In this way, the dance functions as ______.`, o: [`a way to rest`, `a defense against enemies`, `a mating ritual`, `a form of communication`], a: 3,
      why: `춤이 먹이 위치를 '알려 준다(tell)'고 했으므로 의사소통의 한 형태이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Chess masters can glance at a board for a few seconds and then reproduce the positions of nearly all the pieces. At first, this ability seems to reflect an extraordinary memory. Yet when the pieces are placed randomly, in arrangements that could never occur in a real game, the masters perform no better than beginners. Their advantage, it turns out, lies not in remembering more pieces but in seeing the board differently. Through years of study, they have stored thousands of familiar patterns, so that what a novice sees as twenty separate pieces, a master sees as a few meaningful groups. This explains why expertise in one field rarely transfers to another: a chess master's patterns are of little use when reading a medical scan. Expertise, in this sense, is less about general mental power than about <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`, o: [`the ability to memorize random information`, `speed in calculating many moves ahead`, `knowledge organized into recognizable units`, `confidence gained through competition`], a: 2,
      why: `무작위 배치에서는 초보자와 같다는 점, 수천 개의 익숙한 패턴을 의미 있는 묶음으로 본다는 점이 근거이다. 무작위 정보 암기는 오히려 반대 내용이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">No single person knows how to make a pencil from start to finish. The wood comes from forests in one country, the graphite from mines in another, and the rubber from plantations far away. Thousands of workers who will never meet contribute to the final product, and most of them have no idea that their labor ends up in a pencil. How is such cooperation achieved without anyone directing it? Many economists point to prices. When a material becomes scarce, its price rises, and users everywhere are encouraged to economize on it, even if they do not know why it has become scarce. Producers, in turn, are motivated to supply more. No central planner issues these instructions; each person reacts only to the prices in front of him or her. In this way, prices <span class="blank"></span>, allowing millions of separate decisions to fit together.</div>`, o: [`transmit information that no individual possesses`, `reflect the personal values of consumers`, `prevent producers from making profits`, `depend on decisions made by governments`], a: 0,
      why: `희소한 이유를 모르는 사람도 가격만 보고 행동을 조정하므로, 가격은 누구도 혼자 갖지 못한 정보를 전달한다. 정부 결정은 '지휘하는 사람 없이'와 반대된다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Sketches by great painters are often more moving than their finished works. A few quick lines suggesting a face or a gesture can seem more alive than a carefully polished portrait. One explanation is that an incomplete image <span class="blank"></span>. When the outline of a figure is only hinted at, the viewer's mind fills in the missing details, drawing on personal memories and expectations. The resulting image is partly created by the viewer and therefore feels personal and immediate. Psychologists have found that people looking at such images spend more time exploring them, as if solving a small puzzle. A finished painting, by contrast, has already made every decision, leaving the viewer with less to do. This may explain why some artists deliberately left parts of their works unfinished, trusting the audience to complete them.</div>`, o: [`reveals the technical weaknesses of the artist`, `is easier to sell than a finished one`, `shows exactly what the artist intended`, `invites the viewer to participate in its creation`], a: 3,
      why: `감상자가 빠진 부분을 채우며 이미지가 '부분적으로 감상자에 의해 만들어진다'는 문장이 재진술 단서이다. '의도를 정확히 보여 준다'는 완성작에 해당한다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Scientific models are often criticized for being unrealistic. Economists assume that people are perfectly rational; physicists treat planets as points with no size; biologists study populations in which every individual is identical. None of these assumptions is true, yet the models built on them have produced valuable insights. The reason is that a model's purpose is not to copy reality but to isolate a few factors so that their effects can be understood clearly. A model that included every detail of the real world would be as complicated as the world itself and would explain nothing. The art of modeling, then, lies in <span class="blank"></span>. A good model, like a good caricature, exaggerates what matters and ignores the rest. Its value lies not in how closely it resembles reality but in how much it helps us understand.</div>`, o: [`collecting as much data as possible`, `deciding wisely what to leave out`, `making assumptions that are literally true`, `avoiding any kind of simplification`], a: 1,
      why: `모든 세부를 담은 모델은 아무것도 설명하지 못하며, 좋은 모델은 중요한 것만 과장하고 나머지는 무시한다고 했다. 즉 무엇을 뺄지 잘 정하는 것이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">In small villages, people rarely needed formal contracts. Everyone knew everyone else, and a person who cheated a neighbor would quickly lose the trust of the entire community. Reputation, in other words, served as a powerful guarantee of good behavior. As societies grew larger and trade extended to strangers, this informal system began to break down. A merchant dealing with a buyer from a distant city could cheat without facing any social consequences at home. The buyer, in turn, had no way of knowing whether the merchant could be trusted. New institutions such as courts, written contracts, and later credit ratings and online reviews emerged to solve this problem. Each of them, in its own way, makes a stranger's past behavior visible or punishable. Seen in this light, many modern institutions can be understood as attempts to <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`, o: [`eliminate the need for trust in trade`, `protect merchants from government control`, `recreate the effects of reputation among strangers`, `return society to the size of a village`], a: 2,
      why: `마을에서 평판이 하던 역할(과거 행동이 알려지고 처벌됨)을 낯선 사람들 사이에서 제도가 대신한다. 신뢰의 필요를 없애는 것이 아니라 신뢰를 가능하게 하는 것이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We tend to think of memory as a recording that can be replayed whenever we wish. Research, however, shows that remembering is closer to rebuilding than to replaying. Each time we recall an event, we reconstruct it from fragments, filling the gaps with what we expect or what we have learned since. Because of this, a memory can change every time it is retrieved. Details suggested by others may be absorbed into the memory and later feel completely genuine. In laboratory studies, people have even come to remember events that never happened after being repeatedly asked about them. This is why eyewitnesses who are confident are not necessarily accurate. Their confidence often grows each time they retell the story, even as the details drift further from what actually occurred. Paradoxically, the memories we revisit most often may be <span class="blank"></span>, since each retrieval offers another opportunity for alteration.</div>`, o: [`the ones that have changed the most`, `the most precisely preserved`, `the least emotionally important`, `the easiest to forget`], a: 0,
      why: `회상할 때마다 기억이 변할 기회가 생기므로 가장 자주 떠올린 기억이 가장 많이 변했을 수 있다. 'Paradoxically'는 상식(자주 떠올리면 정확하다)과 반대라는 신호이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We usually regard difficulty as an obstacle to be removed. Technologies are praised for making tasks easier, and products are designed to require as little effort as possible. In most areas of life, this is a genuine improvement: few people miss washing clothes by hand. Yet some activities lose their value precisely when they become effortless. A mountain climbed by helicopter offers a view but not an achievement; a puzzle whose solution is printed beside it offers an answer but not the pleasure of solving. In such cases, the difficulty is not a cost we pay to reach the goal but part of what makes the goal worth reaching. This suggests that the elimination of effort, while often welcome, can <span class="blank"></span>. Before simplifying every task, we might ask whether the struggle itself was what we valued.</div>`, o: [`make achievements more satisfying`, `strip certain experiences of their meaning`, `increase the number of goals we can reach`, `reveal the true purpose of technology`], a: 1,
      why: `헬리콥터 등반·답이 인쇄된 퍼즐처럼 노력이 사라지면 성취와 즐거움이 사라진다. 즉 경험의 의미를 빼앗을 수 있다. ①은 반대 방향이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Organizations often rely on numbers to judge performance. Schools are evaluated by test scores, hospitals by waiting times, and police departments by crime statistics. Such measures are useful because they summarize complex activities in simple terms. They allow managers to compare performance across many units at a glance. Problems arise, however, when the numbers become targets that people are rewarded for reaching. Teachers may then focus on test preparation rather than genuine understanding, and hospitals may reclassify patients to shorten official waiting times without improving care. In each case, the number improves while the quality it was meant to reflect stays the same or even declines. The lesson is that a measure <span class="blank"></span>: once people begin to aim at it directly, it loses its connection to what it was supposed to indicate.</div>`, o: [`should be changed every year`, `must be kept secret from the public`, `is more reliable than human judgment`, `works best when it is not the goal itself`], a: 3,
      why: `콜론 뒤 '사람들이 그것을 직접 겨냥하기 시작하면 의미를 잃는다'가 재진술이다. 따라서 지표는 목표 자체가 아닐 때 가장 잘 작동한다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">The history of science is often told as a series of correct discoveries, but mistaken theories have played a surprisingly productive role. The belief that heat was an invisible fluid, for example, was eventually abandoned, yet experiments designed to measure this fluid produced precise data that later supported the modern theory of energy. Similarly, astronomers who searched for a planet that did not exist developed observational techniques that proved valuable for other purposes. In both cases, the theory was wrong, but the work it inspired was not wasted. A wrong theory that is clear enough to be tested pushes researchers to make careful measurements, and these measurements outlast the theory itself. In this sense, scientific progress depends less on <span class="blank"></span> than on proposing ideas precise enough to be proven wrong.</div>`, o: [`collecting reliable data`, `designing careful experiments`, `avoiding errors from the start`, `questioning established theories`], a: 2,
      why: `틀린 이론도 정밀한 측정을 이끌어 진보에 기여했다는 글이므로, 진보는 '처음부터 오류를 피하는 것'보다 반증 가능한 명확한 생각을 내놓는 데 달려 있다. 신뢰할 만한 자료 수집은 오히려 틀린 이론이 낳은 성과로 제시된다.` },
  ],
});

ENG.add('h3', '순서 배열·문장 삽입 고난도', {
  sub: '지시어·연결어·관사로 글의 연결 고리를 찾는다',
  kw: ['순서 배열', '문장 삽입', '지시어', '연결어', '대명사', '관사', '논리적 단절'],
  sections: [
    { h: '순서 배열 전략',
      p: `순서 배열은 주어진 글 뒤에 (A)(B)(C)를 이어 붙이는 문제이다. 각 단락의 <b>첫 문장</b>에 있는 지시어·연결어·대명사가 앞 단락의 <b>마지막 문장</b>과 연결되는지를 본다. 내용 추측보다 <span class="hl">연결 단서</span>를 우선한다.`,
      table: { head: ['단서', '예', '판단'], rows: [
        ['지시어', 'This belief, Such a view, These cells', '앞에 그 내용이 이미 나와야 함'],
        ['대명사', 'it, they, its', '가리키는 명사가 앞에 있어야 함'],
        ['관사', 'a stone → the stone', '처음 나올 때 a, 다시 나올 때 the'],
        ['연결어', 'However, In this way, Then', '대조·결론·시간 순서 확인'],
      ], cap: '첫 문장의 단서를 앞 단락의 마지막 문장과 맞춘다' },
      ex: [['A scientist found a strange stone. The stone was a meteorite.', '한 과학자가 이상한 돌을 발견했다. 그 돌은 운석이었다.'],
           ['Freed from this task, painters began to explore new styles.', '이 일에서 벗어나자 화가들은 새로운 양식을 탐구하기 시작했다.']] },
    { h: '문장 삽입 전략',
      p: `문장 삽입은 글에서 <b>논리가 끊기는 곳</b>을 찾는 문제이다. 주어진 문장의 단서(대명사, however, also 등)를 먼저 분석한 뒤, 본문을 읽으며 <span class="hl">앞뒤 연결이 어색한 지점</span>을 찾는다. 그 자리에 주어진 문장을 넣으면 앞뒤가 모두 자연스러워져야 한다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일', '체크 포인트'], rows: [
        ['1', '주어진 문장의 단서 표시', 'The survivors, however, by contrast …'],
        ['2', '본문에서 갑자기 화제가 바뀌는 곳 찾기', '대명사가 가리킬 대상이 없는 문장'],
        ['3', '그 바로 앞 자리에 넣어 보기', '앞 문장 → 주어진 문장 → 뒤 문장'],
        ['4', '다른 자리와 비교', '두 곳이 가능해 보이면 단서를 다시 확인'],
      ] },
      ex: [['The survivors, however, carry traits that resist the drug.', '그러나 살아남은 세균은 약에 저항하는 특성을 지닌다.'],
           ['In larger groups, by contrast, each contribution is harder to identify.', '반대로 큰 집단에서는 각자의 기여를 파악하기 어렵다.']] },
    { h: '자주 나오는 함정',
      p: `고난도 문항은 <b>비슷한 내용이 두 번 나오는</b> 구조를 써서 혼동을 노린다. 내용이 비슷해 보여도 지시어가 가리키는 대상이 정확히 일치하는지 확인해야 한다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정', '모습', '대처'], rows: [
        ['내용만 보고 배열', '주제가 같아 어느 순서도 가능해 보임', '지시어·대명사 대상으로 결정'],
        ['However의 방향', '대조 대상이 앞 단락에 없음', '무엇과 무엇의 대조인지 표시'],
        ['예시의 위치', 'For example이 일반 진술보다 먼저 옴', '일반 진술 → 예시 순서 확인'],
        ['결론 단락', 'In this way, Seen in this light', '대개 마지막에 위치'],
      ] },
      warn: '주어진 문장이 결론처럼 보여도 그 안에 this/these가 있으면 앞 내용을 받는 중간 문장일 수 있다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `빈칸에 알맞은 것은?<br>A scientist found a strange stone. ______ stone was later identified as a meteorite.`, o: [`The`, `A`, `Another`, `Any`], a: 0,
      why: `앞에서 이미 언급된 돌을 다시 가리키므로 정관사 The가 맞다. 순서 배열에서 a → the는 강력한 단서이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `다음 문장 <b>바로 앞에</b> 올 내용으로 가장 알맞은 것은?<br>As a result, the river flooded the nearby villages.`, o: [`The villagers built new houses.`, `The river is very long.`, `Floods are rare in the region.`, `It rained heavily for a week.`], a: 3,
      why: `As a result는 원인 뒤에 결과를 잇는다. 홍수의 원인인 '일주일간 폭우'가 앞에 와야 한다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `자연스러운 순서는?<br>(A) But this simple act changed his life.<br>(B) One day, a young clerk picked up a book someone had left on a train.<br>(C) Reading it, he discovered a passion for astronomy that later made him famous.`, o: [`(B)-(A)-(C)`, `(A)-(B)-(C)`, `(C)-(A)-(B)`, `(A)-(C)-(B)`], a: 0,
      why: `(B)에서 책을 줍는 행동이 나오고, (A)의 this simple act가 그것을 받으며, (C)의 it(책)을 읽은 결과로 이어진다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?<br><b>Unfortunately, the supplies never arrived.</b><br>( ① ) The expedition team planned to cross the glacier in ten days. ( ② ) They arranged for food to be dropped by plane halfway along the route. ( ③ ) Forced to ration what little they had, the team turned back before reaching their goal. ( ④ )`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 2,
      why: `식량 투하를 준비했다는 문장 뒤, 남은 식량을 아껴 먹어야 했다는 문장 앞에 '보급품이 오지 않았다'가 와야 인과가 이어진다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `주어진 글 다음에 이어질 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<br><b>Plants cannot move to find food, so they must make it themselves.</b><br>(A) This process, called photosynthesis, also releases oxygen into the air.<br>(B) They use sunlight to turn water and carbon dioxide into sugar.<br>(C) Without it, most animals, including humans, could not breathe.`, o: [`(A)-(C)-(B)`, `(B)-(A)-(C)`, `(B)-(C)-(A)`, `(C)-(B)-(A)`], a: 1,
      why: `(B)가 먹이를 만드는 방법을 설명하고, (A)의 This process가 그것을 받으며, (C)의 it(산소)이 (A)의 oxygen을 가리킨다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `주어진 문장이 <b>In contrast, …</b>로 시작할 때 그 앞에 와야 하는 내용은?`, o: [`주어진 문장과 반대되는 성질의 설명`, `주어진 문장의 구체적인 예`, `주어진 문장의 결과`, `주어진 문장과 같은 내용의 반복`], a: 0,
      why: `In contrast(대조적으로)는 앞 내용과 반대되는 내용을 이끈다. 따라서 앞에는 대조되는 성질이 먼저 나와야 한다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage"><b>A placebo is a treatment with no active ingredient, such as a sugar pill.</b><br>(A) Surprisingly, patients who receive one often report that their symptoms improve. In some studies of pain, the effect has been nearly as strong as that of standard medication. Such results have been reported for headaches, nausea, and even fatigue.<br>(B) Interestingly, the effect seems to depend on details that should not matter. Larger pills, for example, tend to work better than smaller ones, and two pills a day work better than one, further evidence that belief, not chemistry, is doing the work.<br>(C) This improvement cannot be explained by the pill itself, so it must come from the patient's expectations. Researchers believe these expectations trigger real changes in the body, including the release of natural painkillers.</div>`, o: [`(A)-(C)-(B)`, `(B)-(A)-(C)`, `(B)-(C)-(A)`, `(C)-(B)-(A)`], a: 0,
      why: `(A)의 one이 주어진 글의 a placebo를 받고, (C)의 This improvement가 (A)의 증상 개선을 받으며, (B)는 기대(belief)가 작용한다는 (C)의 설명에 추가 증거를 더한다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?<div class="passage"><b>This is true even if one of them is better at producing both goods.</b><br>Economists have argued for centuries that trade benefits all its participants, not just the strongest. Trade allows countries to specialize in what they do relatively well. ( ① ) Suppose two countries can each produce both rice and computers. ( ② ) If each focuses on the good it produces at a lower opportunity cost and trades for the other, both can end up with more of each good than before. ( ③ ) What matters is not absolute skill but relative cost: what a country must give up to produce something. ( ④ ) A highly productive country still benefits from importing goods whose production would draw its workers away from industries where its advantage is even greater. This is why economists see trade as a source of mutual benefit rather than a contest.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 2,
      why: `'두 나라 모두 이익'이라는 문장 뒤에 '한 나라가 두 상품 모두 더 잘 만들어도 그렇다'가 오고, 이어서 '중요한 것은 절대적 능력이 아니라 상대적 비용'이 그 이유를 설명한다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage"><b>When photography was invented in the nineteenth century, many painters feared that their art would become unnecessary.</b><br>(A) Freed from this task, painters began to explore what a camera could not do. They experimented with bold colors, visible brushstrokes, and distorted forms that expressed feelings rather than appearances. Some even abandoned realistic perspective altogether.<br>(B) In this way, a technology that seemed to threaten painting ended up pushing it in new directions. Many of the movements of modern art can be seen as responses to the camera. Photography, in short, did not end painting; it gave painting new freedom.<br>(C) Their fear was understandable. For centuries, one of painting's main functions had been to record how people and places looked, and a camera could do this faster and more accurately.</div>`, o: [`(A)-(C)-(B)`, `(B)-(A)-(C)`, `(B)-(C)-(A)`, `(C)-(A)-(B)`], a: 3,
      why: `(C)의 Their fear가 주어진 글의 화가들의 두려움을 받고, (A)의 this task는 (C)의 '모습을 기록하는 일'을 가리키며, (B)의 In this way가 결론을 맺는다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?<div class="passage"><b>The survivors, however, carry whatever genetic traits allowed them to resist the drug.</b><br>Antibiotics have saved millions of lives, but their effectiveness is steadily declining. Some bacteria that once responded to common drugs now survive even the strongest treatments available. Infections that were once easily cured have become dangerous again. The reason lies in natural selection. ( ① ) When a population of bacteria is exposed to an antibiotic, most of the cells die. ( ② ) Because bacteria reproduce rapidly, these resistant cells can soon give rise to an entire population that the drug can no longer control. ( ③ ) Overuse makes the problem worse: every unnecessary prescription gives resistant bacteria another opportunity to outcompete their vulnerable neighbors. ( ④ ) For this reason, doctors are now urged to prescribe antibiotics only when they are truly needed, and researchers are searching for entirely new kinds of drugs.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 1,
      why: `'대부분의 세포가 죽는다' 뒤에 however로 '살아남은 것들'이 대조되고, 그 뒤 these resistant cells가 주어진 문장의 survivors를 가리킨다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Imagine a wooden ship whose planks are replaced one by one as they decay, until none of the original wood remains.</b><br>(A) Now suppose someone collected all the discarded planks and rebuilt them into a second ship. Which of the two is the original? Both seem to have a reasonable claim, yet they cannot both be the same ship.<br>(B) Is it still the same ship? Most people would say yes, since the change happened gradually and the ship kept its shape and function throughout.<br>(C) Puzzles like this are not merely games. They force us to ask what makes anything, whether a city, an institution, or even a person whose cells are constantly renewed, remain itself over time. There may be no single correct answer, but asking the question reveals what we really mean by identity.</div>`, o: [`(A)-(C)-(B)`, `(B)-(A)-(C)`, `(C)-(A)-(B)`, `(C)-(B)-(A)`], a: 1,
      why: `(B)의 it이 주어진 글의 배를 받고 첫 질문을 던지며, (A)의 Now suppose가 상황을 한 단계 더 꼬고, (C)의 Puzzles like this가 앞의 사고 실험 전체를 정리한다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?<div class="passage"><b>In larger groups, by contrast, each member's contribution becomes harder to identify.</b><br>It might seem that people working together would always achieve more than the same people working alone. Experiments, however, reveal a curious pattern. ( ① ) When individuals pull on a rope as part of a team, each person exerts less force than when pulling alone, and the decline grows as the team gets bigger. ( ② ) Psychologists explain this in terms of accountability. ( ③ ) When working alone or in a pair, a person knows that any lack of effort will be noticed. ( ④ ) Members therefore feel that their effort matters less, and they unconsciously reduce it. The effect can be weakened, though, when each person's work is made visible or when the task is personally meaningful. Simply telling team members that their individual results will be measured is often enough to restore effort.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `'혼자나 둘이면 노력 부족이 드러난다'와 대조(by contrast)되어 큰 집단 이야기가 오고, 이어서 Members therefore …가 큰 집단 구성원의 반응을 설명한다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage"><b>A piece of paper money has almost no value as a physical object.</b><br>(A) Money, then, is less a thing than a shared agreement. When that agreement weakens, as during periods of extreme inflation, the paper quickly returns to being what it physically always was. Money works only as long as people trust that it will keep working.<br>(B) This belief, in turn, rests on the expectation that others will continue to accept it. Each person values money only because everyone else does. A shopkeeper accepts a banknote because she expects her supplier to accept it, and the supplier expects the same of others.<br>(C) Yet people willingly exchange hours of work for it. They do so because they believe it can later be exchanged for food, housing, or other goods. Without this belief, no one would give up anything of value in exchange for paper.</div>`, o: [`(A)-(C)-(B)`, `(B)-(C)-(A)`, `(C)-(A)-(B)`, `(C)-(B)-(A)`], a: 3,
      why: `(C)의 Yet이 '물리적 가치가 없다'와 대조되고, (B)의 This belief가 (C)의 '교환할 수 있다는 믿음'을 받으며, (A)의 then이 결론(공유된 합의)을 맺는다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `글의 흐름으로 보아, 주어진 문장이 들어가기에 가장 적절한 곳은?<div class="passage"><b>Listeners began to expect every live performance to match this flawless standard.</b><br>Before the invention of recording, music existed only in the moment of performance. ( ① ) Each concert was unique, and small mistakes were quickly forgotten. Audiences heard a piece as it happened, with all its imperfections, and then it was gone. ( ② ) Recording technology changed this situation dramatically. Engineers could combine the best parts of several takes, producing versions of a piece without a single wrong note. ( ③ ) As a result, performers came under new pressure to play with mechanical precision, sometimes at the cost of spontaneity and risk. ( ④ ) Some critics argue that this shift has made concerts safer but less exciting, as musicians avoid the bold choices that might lead to errors. Others reply that the best performers still find ways to surprise their audiences.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 2,
      why: `this flawless standard는 '틀린 음 하나 없는 녹음'을 가리키므로 그 뒤에 와야 하고, 청중의 기대가 높아진 결과(As a result) 연주자가 압박을 받게 된다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `주어진 글 다음에 이어질 글의 순서로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Sleep presents a puzzle for evolutionary biologists.</b><br>(A) Dolphins offer a striking example. Because they must surface regularly to breathe, losing consciousness entirely could be fatal. Their solution is remarkable: one half of the brain sleeps while the other half stays alert. Some birds appear to use a similar strategy during long flights over the ocean.<br>(B) An animal that is asleep cannot search for food, care for its young, or watch for predators. If sleep were not essential, natural selection should have eliminated it long ago. Yet every animal studied so far appears to sleep in some form.<br>(C) The fact that it has survived suggests that it performs functions too important to give up. Indeed, even animals for which sleeping is especially dangerous have found ways to do it.</div>`, o: [`(A)-(C)-(B)`, `(B)-(C)-(A)`, `(C)-(A)-(B)`, `(C)-(B)-(A)`], a: 1,
      why: `(B)가 수수께끼(잠의 위험)를 구체화하고, (C)가 그럼에도 잠이 살아남은 의미를 말하며 '잠이 특히 위험한 동물'을 언급하고, (A)가 그 예(돌고래)를 든다.` },
  ],
});

ENG.add('h3', '주제·요지·제목 실전', {
  sub: '주제문을 찾고 선지의 범위를 판정한다',
  kw: ['주제', '요지', '제목', '주제문', '두괄식·미괄식', '일반화', '범위'],
  sections: [
    { h: '주제문 찾기',
      p: `대의 파악 문제는 <b>주제문</b>을 찾으면 거의 풀린다. 주제문은 글의 처음(두괄식)이나 끝(미괄식)에 많지만, 고난도 지문은 <span class="hl">통념 → 반박(However)</span> 구조로 중간에 나오기도 한다. 예시·실험·수치는 주제문을 뒷받침하는 근거일 뿐이다.`,
      table: { head: ['글의 구조', '주제문 위치', '신호'], rows: [
        ['두괄식', '첫 문장', '일반적 진술로 시작'],
        ['미괄식', '마지막 문장', 'Therefore, In short, then'],
        ['통념 반박형', 'However, Yet 뒤', 'We usually think … / Many believe …'],
        ['질문 제시형', '질문에 대한 답', 'Why …? → The answer is …'],
      ], cap: '통념 반박형에서는 앞의 통념이 오답 선지로 나온다' },
      ex: [['Many people believe that talent decides success. Yet …', '많은 사람이 재능이 성공을 결정한다고 믿는다. 그러나 …(통념 → 반박)'],
           ['Allowing ourselves to be bored may be a necessary step toward creativity.', '때로 지루함을 허용하는 것이 창의성으로 가는 필수 단계일 수 있다.']] },
    { h: '주제·요지·제목의 차이',
      p: `세 유형 모두 글의 중심 내용을 묻지만 표현 방식이 다르다. <b>주제</b>는 영어 명사구, <b>요지</b>는 우리말 문장(필자의 주장), <b>제목</b>은 압축되고 때로 비유적인 영어 표현이다.`,
      table: { head: ['유형', '선지 형태', '판단 요령'], rows: [
        ['주제', 'the effects of … / why …', '핵심 소재 + 필자의 관점이 모두 들어 있는가'],
        ['요지', '~해야 한다 / ~이다', '필자가 결국 하고 싶은 말인가'],
        ['제목', 'A: B 형식, 의문문, 비유', '비유를 풀어 주제와 같은지 확인'],
      ] },
      ex: [['Diversity: Insurance for Our Food Supply', '다양성: 식량 공급을 위한 보험 (비유형 제목)'],
           ['Why a Perfect Copy Is Not Worth the Same', '완벽한 복제품이 같은 가치를 갖지 못하는 이유 (why형 제목)']] },
    { h: '오답 선지의 유형',
      p: `대의 파악의 오답은 대부분 <b>범위</b>가 맞지 않는다. 지문의 한 예시만 다루면 너무 좁고, 지문에 없는 영역까지 넓히면 너무 넓다. 또 통념을 그대로 옮긴 선지는 반대 방향 오답이다.`,
      table: { head: ['오답 유형', '예', '판정'], rows: [
        ['너무 좁음', '공장 근처 주민의 건강 위험', '예시 하나만 다룸'],
        ['너무 넓음', '경제학의 역사', '글보다 큰 범위'],
        ['반대 방향', '멀티태스킹은 연습으로 향상된다', '필자의 주장과 반대'],
        ['언급 없음', '정부가 세금을 폐지해야 한다', '지문에 근거 없음'],
      ] },
      tip: '정답 선지를 고른 뒤 “이 글의 모든 단락이 이 말을 뒷받침하는가?”를 확인한다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `다음 글의 주제문은?<br>①&nbsp;Colors can influence how food tastes to us. ②&nbsp;In one test, people rated the same drink as sweeter when it was colored red. ③&nbsp;Others found a yellow drink more sour. ④&nbsp;The researchers used drinks that had no artificial flavor.`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 0,
      why: `①이 일반적 진술이고 ②③은 실험 예시, ④는 실험 조건에 관한 세부 사항이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `나머지를 모두 포괄하는 가장 일반적인 진술은?`, o: [`Bees pollinate fruit trees.`, `Insects play essential roles in ecosystems.`, `Beetles break down dead wood.`, `Ants spread plant seeds.`], a: 1,
      why: `벌·딱정벌레·개미의 역할은 모두 '곤충이 생태계에서 중요한 역할을 한다'의 구체적 예이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `다음 중 필자의 주장(요지)을 드러내는 표현이 <b>아닌</b> 것은?`, o: [`We should …`, `It is essential to …`, `For example, …`, `It is time to …`], a: 2,
      why: `For example은 예시를 이끄는 표현이다. 나머지는 당위·주장을 나타내는 전형적인 요지 신호이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<br>Many people believe that talent decides success. Yet studies of top performers show that most of them practiced deliberately for years before excelling. Even those considered "gifted" rarely succeeded without sustained effort.`, o: [`재능은 성공과 전혀 관계가 없다.`, `연구자들은 재능을 측정하지 못한다.`, `최고 수준의 사람들은 연습을 싫어한다.`, `꾸준하고 의도적인 연습이 성공에 결정적이다.`], a: 3,
      why: `통념(재능) 뒤 Yet으로 연습의 중요성을 주장한다. '전혀 관계없다'는 지나친 일반화이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<br>When cities turned off streetlights late at night to save energy, some residents worried about crime. Surprisingly, in many areas crime did not rise, and the darker skies helped migrating birds and night-flying insects.`, o: [`Why Crime Rises After Dark`, `Darkness: A Hidden Benefit for Cities and Nature`, `How Streetlights Were Invented`, `Birds That Prefer Bright Cities`], a: 1,
      why: `가로등을 끄자 범죄는 늘지 않고 자연에 도움이 되었다는 내용이므로 '어둠의 숨은 이점'이 맞다. ①은 지문과 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<br>Sharing a meal has long been a way to build relationships. Eating together slows people down, encourages conversation, and creates a sense of belonging. It is no accident that many cultures mark important events with feasts.`, o: [`the nutritional value of feasts`, `the history of cooking`, `social functions of eating together`, `ways to eat more slowly`], a: 2,
      why: `함께 먹는 것이 관계·대화·소속감을 만든다는 사회적 기능이 주제이다. ④는 세부 하나만 다룬 좁은 선지이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Boredom is usually treated as something to be avoided, and modern technology offers countless ways to escape it. Yet researchers increasingly argue that boredom serves a useful purpose. When we feel bored, the mind signals that our current activity is not satisfying our needs, prompting us to look for something more meaningful. In experiments, participants who first completed a dull task later produced more creative ideas than those who had not, as if the empty time had pushed their thoughts to wander into new territory. By filling every idle moment with a screen, we may be silencing this signal before it can do its work. Allowing ourselves to be bored occasionally, then, may not be a waste of time but a necessary step toward creativity and self-direction.</div>`, o: [`지루함은 더 의미 있는 활동과 창의성을 이끄는 신호이므로 때로 허용할 필요가 있다.`, `지루한 과제는 학습 효율을 떨어뜨리므로 줄여야 한다.`, `전자 기기는 창의적 사고를 돕는 유용한 도구이다.`, `창의성은 타고나는 것이어서 환경의 영향을 받지 않는다.`], a: 0,
      why: `Yet 뒤에 지루함의 유용성이 제시되고 마지막 문장(then)에서 지루함을 허용하라고 정리한다. ②는 통념 쪽, ③은 화면이 신호를 막는다는 내용과 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When a factory releases smoke into the air, the people living nearby suffer health problems, yet the factory does not pay for this harm. Its products therefore appear cheaper than they really are, because part of their cost is borne by others. Economists call such side effects externalities, and they can also be positive: a person who restores an old building improves the neighborhood for everyone, but receives no payment from those who benefit. In both cases, the market price fails to reflect the full consequences of an activity. As a result, harmful activities tend to be overproduced and beneficial ones underproduced. Taxes on pollution and subsidies for public goods are attempts to correct this gap, bringing private incentives closer to social costs and benefits. Without such corrections, markets can produce outcomes that no one would deliberately choose.</div>`, o: [`the health risks of living near factories`, `how to restore old buildings economically`, `the reasons governments should abolish taxes`, `why market prices can fail to reflect the true costs and benefits of activities`], a: 3,
      why: `공장과 건물 복원은 외부 효과의 두 예시이고, 핵심은 '시장 가격이 활동의 전체 결과를 반영하지 못한다'이다. ①②는 예시 하나만 다룬 좁은 선지이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Suppose a painting admired for decades as a masterpiece is suddenly revealed to be a forgery. Nothing about the canvas has changed: the colors, the brushwork, and the composition are exactly as before. Yet its price collapses, and museum visitors who once stood before it in admiration now pass it by. This reaction raises an intriguing question about what we value in art. If our appreciation were purely visual, the discovery should make no difference. The fact that it does suggests that we value artworks partly for their history: the knowledge that a particular person, at a particular moment, made these marks. In this sense, a painting is not only an image but also a record of human achievement, and a forgery, however skillful, cannot share that story.</div>`, o: [`How to Detect Forged Paintings`, `Why a Perfect Copy Is Not Worth the Same`, `The Rising Prices of Modern Art`, `Museums: Places for Visual Pleasure Only`], a: 1,
      why: `겉모습이 같아도 위작이 가치를 잃는 이유(작품의 역사·인간의 성취)를 다룬다. ①은 위작 감별법으로 언급되지 않았고, ④는 '순수하게 시각적이지 않다'는 내용과 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Scientists are trained to express uncertainty. They speak of probabilities, confidence intervals, and the limits of their data. To the public, however, such caution can sound like weakness or confusion. When one study suggests that coffee is harmful and another that it is beneficial, many people conclude that scientists do not know anything. In fact, disagreement and revision are signs that science is working as intended: claims are tested, challenged, and refined as evidence accumulates. A conclusion that has survived many such challenges deserves far more trust than one that has never been tested. The problem lies less in science itself than in how its findings are communicated. Reports that present single studies as final truths set the public up for disappointment. If people understood that knowledge grows gradually through correction, they would be less likely to dismiss science whenever its conclusions change.</div>`, o: [`과학자들은 불확실성을 표현하지 말고 확정적으로 말해야 한다.`, `커피의 건강 효과에 관한 연구는 신뢰할 수 없다.`, `과학 지식이 수정을 통해 점진적으로 발전한다는 점을 대중에게 전달해야 한다.`, `언론은 과학 연구를 보도하지 않는 것이 바람직하다.`], a: 2,
      why: `문제는 과학이 아니라 전달 방식에 있으며, 지식이 수정을 통해 자란다는 점을 이해시키면 불신이 줄어든다는 것이 요지이다. ①은 단일 연구를 최종 진리처럼 전하는 태도로 필자가 비판하는 쪽이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We usually think of tools as servants of our intentions: we decide what to do, and the tool helps us do it. Philosophers of technology, however, argue that the relationship runs in both directions. A person holding a hammer begins to see nails everywhere; a person with a camera starts to notice scenes as potential photographs. Tools do not merely carry out our purposes but quietly reshape what we pay attention to and what we consider worth doing. Maps changed how people imagined territory, and clocks changed how they experienced time, dividing the day into equal units that had little to do with the sun. Understanding this influence does not require rejecting technology, but it does call for asking, with each new tool, how it might be changing us.</div>`, o: [`the way tools influence human perception and values`, `the history of the invention of clocks and maps`, `the reasons people should avoid new technologies`, `the difficulty of learning to use complex tools`], a: 0,
      why: `통념(도구는 의도의 하인) 뒤 however로 도구가 우리의 주의와 가치를 바꾼다는 주제가 나온다. ③은 '기술을 거부할 필요는 없다'는 마지막 문장과 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Modern farms often plant a single high-yielding variety across vast areas. This approach maximizes production in good years, but it carries a hidden danger. When every plant is genetically similar, a disease that can attack one can attack all of them, and an entire harvest may be lost in a single season. History offers several examples of such failures, some of which led to widespread hunger. Natural ecosystems rarely face this problem to the same degree. In a forest or meadow containing many species and varieties, some individuals are likely to resist any particular threat, allowing the system as a whole to recover. For this reason, agricultural scientists increasingly recommend preserving traditional crop varieties and planting mixtures rather than uniform fields. Diversity may reduce maximum yields slightly, but it acts as a kind of insurance against disaster.</div>`, o: [`How to Maximize Crop Yields`, `Forests Are Disappearing Fast`, `The Origins of Traditional Farming`, `Diversity: Insurance for Our Food Supply`], a: 3,
      why: `마지막 문장의 '다양성은 재난에 대비한 보험'을 압축한 제목이다. ①은 단일 품종 재배의 논리로 필자가 경계하는 쪽이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Many people are proud of their ability to multitask, answering messages while writing reports or listening to lectures while browsing the web. Research on attention, however, suggests that what feels like doing several things at once is usually rapid switching between them. Each switch carries a small cost: the mind must drop one set of rules and load another, and some information is lost in the process. These costs are easy to overlook because each one is tiny, but they accumulate over a day into significant losses of time and accuracy. Ironically, those who multitask most often tend to perform worst on tests of switching, perhaps because frequent practice trains them to respond to every new stimulus rather than to filter out distractions. Doing one thing at a time may feel slower, but it is frequently the faster path to good work.</div>`, o: [`멀티태스킹 능력은 연습을 통해 향상될 수 있다.`, `한 번에 한 가지 일에 집중하는 것이 결국 더 효율적이다.`, `작은 실수는 누적되지 않으므로 신경 쓸 필요가 없다.`, `메시지에 빠르게 답하는 것이 업무 능력의 핵심이다.`], a: 1,
      why: `전환 비용이 쌓이므로 한 가지씩 하는 것이 오히려 빠른 길이라는 것이 요지이다. ①은 멀티태스킹을 자주 하는 사람이 전환 검사에서 가장 못한다는 내용과 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 주제로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Some goods are valued not for what they do but for what they say about their owners. A luxury watch tells time no better than an inexpensive one, yet it signals wealth and taste. Economists call such items positional goods, because their value depends on one's position relative to others. This creates a peculiar dynamic. When everyone's income rises, people spend more on positional goods, but since the goods only matter in comparison, no one ends up better off in relative terms. A household that buys a larger house to keep up with its neighbors may find, a few years later, that the neighbors have done the same. The resulting competition consumes resources without increasing overall satisfaction, much like spectators who all rise from their seats at a concert to see better and end up just as blocked as before.</div>`, o: [`the practical advantages of luxury watches`, `methods for increasing household income`, `the self-defeating nature of competition for relative status`, `the importance of saving money for housing`], a: 2,
      why: `상대적 지위를 위한 경쟁은 모두가 같이 하면 아무도 나아지지 않는 자기 파괴적 성격을 띤다는 것이 주제이다. 시계와 집은 예시일 뿐이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When we look at the night sky, we are not seeing the universe as it is but as it was. Light travels at a finite speed, so the light reaching our eyes from a distant star may have left it thousands of years ago. Some of the stars we admire may no longer exist. The same principle applies, on a much smaller scale, to everything we perceive. Even the face of a person sitting across the table reaches us after a tiny delay, and our brains need additional time to process the signal. Strictly speaking, then, we always experience the world slightly late. Our sense of a shared, immediate present is a convenient construction, assembled from signals that left their sources at different moments. What we call "now" is, in every case, a carefully edited report from the recent past.</div>`, o: [`Living Slightly in the Past`, `How Stars Are Born and Die`, `Why the Brain Processes Faces Quickly`, `The Speed of Sound and Light Compared`], a: 0,
      why: `별빛에서 시작해 모든 지각이 약간 늦게 도착한다는 일반화로 나아가므로 '조금 과거에 사는 우리'가 적절하다. ②③은 세부 소재만 다룬다.` },
  ],
});

ENG.add('h3', '함축 의미 추론', {
  sub: '밑줄 친 비유 표현을 주제와 연결해 해석한다',
  kw: ['함축 의미', '비유', '은유', '주제 연결', '재진술', 'figurative'],
  sections: [
    { h: '함축 의미 풀이 순서',
      p: `함축 의미 문제의 밑줄은 대부분 <b>비유적 표현</b>이며, 글의 주제를 한 번 더 압축한 말이다. 그러므로 밑줄만 따로 해석하지 말고 <span class="hl">글의 주제를 먼저 잡은 뒤 밑줄의 각 부분이 무엇을 가리키는지</span> 짝을 지어 풀어야 한다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일', '체크 포인트'], rows: [
        ['1', '글 전체의 주제 파악', '통념 → 반박, 결론 문장'],
        ['2', '밑줄의 단어를 지문 속 대상과 연결', 'full stories = 내 삶 전체, trailers = 남의 편집된 순간'],
        ['3', '밑줄을 우리말 일반 진술로 풀어 쓰기', '비유를 없애고 의미만 남기기'],
        ['4', '선지와 대조', '주제와 방향이 같은가'],
      ], cap: '밑줄의 단어 하나하나를 지문의 개념과 1:1로 대응시킨다' },
      ex: [['The larger the circle, the longer its edge.', '원이 클수록 둘레가 길다. (아는 것이 많을수록 모르는 것과 맞닿는 부분도 넓다)'],
           ['What looks like fat in good times may be muscle in bad times.', '좋은 시절의 군살이 나쁜 시절엔 근육일 수 있다. (여유분이 위기에 힘이 된다)']] },
    { h: '자주 나오는 비유의 틀',
      p: `수능 함축 의미의 밑줄은 몇 가지 틀로 반복된다. 틀을 알면 해석 방향을 빨리 정할 수 있다.`,
      table: { head: ['비유의 틀', '예', '숨은 의미'], rows: [
        ['역설', 'betray the poem to be faithful to it', '겉으로 반대인 행동이 본질을 지킴'],
        ['신체·건강', 'fat vs muscle', '불필요해 보이는 것 vs 필요한 힘'],
        ['직물·연결', 'pull a single thread', '하나의 변화가 전체에 영향'],
        ['창과 벽', 'both a window and a wall', '드러내면서 동시에 가림'],
        ['소유·거래', 'you may be what is being sold', '사용자의 관심이 상품'],
      ] },
      ex: [['Pulling a single thread can reshape the whole fabric.', '실 한 가닥을 당기면 천 전체가 바뀔 수 있다.'],
           ['Our vocabulary is both a window and a wall.', '어휘는 창이자 벽이다.']] },
    { h: '오답 선지의 함정',
      p: `가장 흔한 오답은 밑줄을 <b>글자 그대로</b> 해석한 선지이다. 예를 들어 “특허 없이 공유해야 한다”, “영화를 덜 봐야 한다”처럼 비유 속 단어를 실제 의미로 받아들인 선지는 거의 오답이다.`,
      table: { head: ['함정', '예', '판정'], rows: [
        ['문자 그대로 해석', 'trailers → 영화 시청', '비유를 풀지 않음'],
        ['지문 단어 반복', '지문 단어만 나열한 선지', '의미가 주제와 다름'],
        ['반대 방향', '오류를 처음부터 피해야 한다', '필자 주장과 반대'],
        ['과장', '모든 가치를 잃는다', 'all, never 등 극단적 표현'],
      ] },
      warn: '밑줄이 역설처럼 보이면 “겉뜻과 속뜻이 반대”라는 신호이다. 겉뜻을 그대로 옮긴 선지를 조심한다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `the tip of the iceberg의 의미로 알맞은 것은?`, o: [`완전히 해결된 문제`, `매우 차가운 태도`, `훨씬 큰 문제의 일부분`, `끝없이 반복되는 일`], a: 2,
      why: `빙산의 일각, 즉 드러난 것은 훨씬 큰 전체의 작은 일부라는 뜻이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `reinvent the wheel의 의미로 알맞은 것은?`, o: [`이미 있는 것을 쓸데없이 다시 만들다`, `새 차를 사다`, `방향을 바꾸다`, `실패를 반복하다`], a: 0,
      why: `바퀴를 다시 발명하다, 즉 이미 존재하는 방법을 불필요하게 처음부터 다시 만든다는 뜻이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `a double-edged sword의 의미로 알맞은 것은?`, o: [`매우 위험한 무기`, `두 사람의 갈등`, `확실한 해결책`, `이점과 위험을 함께 가진 것`], a: 3,
      why: `양날의 검, 즉 도움이 되면서 동시에 해가 될 수도 있는 것을 말한다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `밑줄 친 부분의 의미로 알맞은 것은?<br>For many startups, the first product is only <u>a seed</u>. It is rarely the final version, but it allows the company to learn from real customers.`, o: [`a failed attempt`, `a starting point for later growth`, `a product for farmers`, `a secret plan`], a: 1,
      why: `최종 버전이 아니지만 고객에게서 배우게 해 준다고 했으므로 이후 성장의 출발점이라는 뜻이다. 농부용 제품은 문자 그대로의 해석이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `밑줄 친 부분의 의미로 알맞은 것은?<br>The new law was <u>a bandage on a broken bone</u>: it covered the visible symptoms while the real problem remained untreated.`, o: [`a medical breakthrough`, `a temporary rule for hospitals`, `an inadequate, superficial solution`, `a painful but effective cure`], a: 2,
      why: `콜론 뒤에서 겉 증상만 덮고 진짜 문제는 그대로라고 했으므로 피상적이고 불충분한 해결책이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `밑줄 친 부분의 의미로 알맞은 것은?<br>In debates, he always <u>moves the goalposts</u>: whenever his opponent meets one demand, he raises a new one.`, o: [`changes the standards to avoid admitting defeat`, `plays sports fairly`, `sets clear goals in advance`, `agrees with the opponent quickly`], a: 0,
      why: `상대가 요구를 충족할 때마다 새 요구를 내놓는다는 설명으로 보아 패배를 인정하지 않으려 기준을 바꾼다는 뜻이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">A single experiment, however impressive, rarely settles a scientific question. Results may be distorted by small samples, unnoticed errors, or simple luck. For this reason, scientists place great value on replication: other researchers repeating a study to see whether they obtain the same result. A finding that cannot be reproduced is treated with suspicion, no matter how prestigious the original researchers or journal. In this sense, <u>a discovery belongs to no one until it belongs to everyone</u>. Only when independent groups, using their own methods and materials, arrive at the same conclusion does a claim become part of accepted knowledge. Replication can seem unglamorous, since it rarely produces headlines, but it is the process through which individual observations are transformed into shared and reliable understanding.</div>`, o: [`Scientific discoveries should be shared freely without patents.`, `Famous researchers deserve credit for most discoveries.`, `Scientists should publish their results as quickly as possible.`, `A finding gains authority only when others can confirm it independently.`], a: 3,
      why: `'모두의 것이 된다'는 독립된 연구자들이 재현해 확인한다는 뜻이다. ①은 belong을 소유권(특허)으로 문자 그대로 해석한 오답이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">People often compare their lives with those of others, and social media has multiplied the opportunities for doing so. The trouble is that the comparison is rarely fair. Online, people tend to share their vacations, achievements, and celebrations, while their ordinary worries and failures remain hidden. Viewers, meanwhile, judge these polished images against their own complete experience, including every dull afternoon and private disappointment. One side of the comparison is carefully edited; the other is not edited at all. It is no wonder that many come away feeling that everyone else is happier and more successful. In effect, <u>we are measuring our full stories against other people's trailers</u>. Recognizing this imbalance does not eliminate envy, but it can remind us that the lives we admire from a distance contain the same struggles as our own.</div>`, o: [`We spend too much time watching films instead of living.`, `We judge our whole lives against others' carefully selected best moments.`, `We share our private problems more than others do.`, `We prefer to watch others rather than act ourselves.`], a: 1,
      why: `full stories는 지루한 순간까지 포함한 내 삶 전체, trailers(예고편)는 남이 골라 올린 멋진 장면이다. ①은 영화를 문자 그대로 해석한 오답이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Many online services cost nothing to use. Their companies, however, are not charities; they earn money by selling advertising, and advertisers pay for one thing above all: the attention of users. To maximize revenue, these services are designed to keep people engaged for as long as possible, using endless feeds, notifications, and recommendations tuned to individual interests. Users may believe they are getting something for free, but they are paying in a currency that is limited and difficult to recover: their time and focus. In this arrangement, <u>if you are not paying for the product, you may be what is being sold</u>. Understanding this does not mean abandoning such services, but it encourages us to ask whose interests are being served each time we open an app.</div>`, o: [`Users' attention is what the companies actually sell to advertisers.`, `Users should pay for services to support companies.`, `Free services are lower in quality than paid ones.`, `Advertisers are the main users of online services.`], a: 0,
      why: `광고주가 사는 것은 사용자의 관심이므로, 무료 사용자는 사실상 판매되는 상품이다. ②는 지문에 없는 주장이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Words allow us to share experience, but they also simplify it. When we call a feeling "anger," we place it in a familiar category, which helps us communicate and respond. Yet no two experiences of anger are identical; each has its own intensity, history, and mixture of other emotions. The label captures what they have in common and discards the rest. Over time, we may come to see our experiences through their labels, noticing only what the words point to. On the other hand, a person who has learned many precise emotional terms may actually perceive finer distinctions in his or her own feelings. In this sense, <u>our vocabulary is both a window and a wall</u>: it opens certain aspects of experience to view while hiding others behind convenient names.</div>`, o: [`Learning new words is difficult for most people.`, `Feelings cannot be described with words at all.`, `Language reveals some parts of experience while concealing others.`, `People with large vocabularies hide their emotions.`], a: 2,
      why: `window는 경험의 일부를 보여 주는 기능, wall은 이름 뒤에 나머지를 숨기는 기능이다. ②는 '전혀 표현할 수 없다'는 과장이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When wolves disappeared from certain parks, the effects reached far beyond the animals they once hunted. Without predators, deer and elk grazed freely along riverbanks, eating young trees before they could grow. The loss of trees led to eroding banks, warmer water, and fewer birds and fish. When wolves were later reintroduced, grazing animals began to avoid open areas where they could easily be caught, and vegetation slowly returned. As trees grew back, riverbanks stabilized, and beavers and songbirds reappeared. Some scientists caution that the full story is more complicated, but the case illustrates an important principle: in an ecosystem, <u>pulling a single thread can reshape the whole fabric</u>. Changes to one species, especially one at the top of the food chain, can ripple outward through many others in ways that are difficult to predict.</div>`, o: [`Scientists can easily predict how ecosystems will respond to change.`, `Wolves should be removed to protect trees and rivers.`, `Each species in an ecosystem exists independently of others.`, `Altering one species can cause wide-reaching effects throughout an ecosystem.`], a: 3,
      why: `실 한 가닥은 한 종(늑대), 천 전체는 생태계를 가리킨다. ①은 '예측하기 어렵다'는 마지막 문장과 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Translating poetry is often described as an impossible task. A poem's meaning is bound up with its sounds, rhythms, and the associations of its words, and these rarely survive the move into another language. A word that rhymes in the original may have no rhyming equivalent; a phrase rich with cultural memory may become flat and literal. Translators must therefore choose what to preserve and what to sacrifice, knowing that something will always be lost. Yet the best translations are not pale copies but new poems in their own right, shaped by the translator's sensibility as well as the original author's. In this sense, <u>a translator must betray the poem in order to be faithful to it</u>: by abandoning the exact words, he or she may capture the spirit that made them matter.</div>`, o: [`Translators should never change any word of the original.`, `Departing from the literal text may be necessary to convey a poem's essence.`, `Most translators intentionally mislead their readers.`, `Poems lose all their value when they are translated.`], a: 1,
      why: `betray는 원문의 단어를 버리는 것, faithful은 시의 정신을 지키는 것이다. 콜론 뒤 설명이 그대로 근거가 된다. ③은 betray를 문자 그대로 해석한 오답이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Most people underestimate how long their tasks will take, even when they have repeatedly been late on similar tasks before. Asked to predict when they will finish a report, they imagine the steps going smoothly and ignore the interruptions, illnesses, and unexpected problems that have delayed them in the past. Psychologists have found that a simple change in perspective reduces this error. Instead of picturing the specific project from the inside, people can ask how long similar projects have actually taken, for themselves or for others. This outside view is less flattering but more accurate, because it includes the delays that optimism tends to erase. The lesson is that, when forecasting our own future, <u>we would do better to consult our history than our hopes</u>.</div>`, o: [`We should base predictions on past outcomes rather than optimistic imagination.`, `We should study history to understand our society.`, `We should give up hope when tasks seem difficult.`, `We should ask others to complete our tasks for us.`], a: 0,
      why: `history는 비슷한 과제에 실제로 걸린 시간(과거 결과), hopes는 일이 순조로울 것이라는 낙관적 상상이다. ②는 history를 역사 공부로 문자 그대로 해석했다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">For decades, businesses pursued efficiency by removing anything that seemed unnecessary. Warehouses were emptied as companies adopted just-in-time delivery, and suppliers were reduced to the cheapest few. In normal times, this approach lowered costs impressively. But when a natural disaster or a sudden surge in demand disrupted supply chains, many firms found that they had no spare parts, no alternative suppliers, and no room to adjust. The very features that had made them efficient made them fragile. Engineers have long understood this trade-off: bridges and aircraft are designed with extra capacity that appears wasteful until the moment it prevents a collapse. <u>What looks like fat in good times may turn out to be muscle in bad times</u>, and organizations that eliminate every margin may save money only until the first crisis arrives.</div>`, o: [`Businesses should always prioritize lowering costs.`, `Healthy diets are important for workers in difficult times.`, `Apparently unnecessary reserves can become vital strengths during a crisis.`, `Engineers design bridges more efficiently than businesses manage supplies.`], a: 2,
      why: `fat은 평소에 낭비처럼 보이는 여유분(재고·여분 공급자), muscle은 위기 때 버티게 하는 힘이다. ②는 비유를 식단으로 문자 그대로 해석한 오답이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 부분이 의미하는 바로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">As knowledge grows, one might expect the list of unanswered questions to shrink. In practice, the opposite often happens. Every discovery tends to raise new questions that could not even have been asked before. When early astronomers learned that the Earth orbits the Sun, they opened up puzzles about gravity, the distances between stars, and the age of the universe. Similarly, decoding the human genome did not end biology's mysteries; it revealed how little we understood about the ways genes interact. A useful image is that of an expanding circle of light in darkness: <u>the larger the circle, the longer its edge</u>. The more we know, the more points of contact we have with what we do not know, and the more clearly we can see the extent of our ignorance.</div>`, o: [`Scientists have now answered most fundamental questions.`, `Ancient astronomers knew more than modern scientists.`, `Knowledge becomes less reliable as it grows larger.`, `Expanding knowledge increases our awareness of what remains unknown.`], a: 3,
      why: `원은 아는 것, 가장자리는 모르는 것과 맞닿는 경계이다. 지식이 늘수록 모르는 것에 대한 인식도 커진다. ①은 '질문이 오히려 늘어난다'는 내용과 반대이다.` },
  ],
});

ENG.add('h3', '도표·안내문·내용 일치', {
  sub: '수치 표현과 세부 정보를 선지와 1:1로 대조한다',
  kw: ['도표', '안내문', '내용 일치', 'percentage point', '배수 표현', '세부 정보'],
  sections: [
    { h: '도표 문제 풀이 순서',
      p: `도표 문제는 영어 독해보다 <b>수치 확인</b>에 가깝다. 먼저 표의 제목·단위·연도를 확인하고, 각 문장의 <span class="hl">비교 표현과 숫자</span>를 하나씩 표에 대조한다. 계산이 필요한 문장(배수, 차이, 합계)이 정답일 가능성이 높으므로 반드시 직접 계산한다.`,
      table: { head: ['표현', '뜻', '확인 방법'], rows: [
        ['twice / three times as … as', '2배 / 3배', '큰 값 ÷ 작은 값'],
        ['more than doubled', '2배 넘게 증가', '나중 값 > 처음 값 × 2'],
        ['by 15 percentage points', '15%p 차이', '30% → 45% (뺄셈)'],
        ['the largest share / the second largest', '가장 큰 / 두 번째로 큰 비율', '해당 연도 안에서 순위'],
        ['less than half of', '절반 미만', '합계와 50 비교'],
      ], cap: '% 끼리의 차이는 percent가 아니라 percentage point로 말한다' },
      ex: [['The share of audiobook users more than doubled.', '오디오북 이용자 비율이 두 배 넘게 늘었다.'],
           ['It rose by 15 percentage points.', '그것은 15%p 상승했다.']] },
    { h: '안내문 문제 풀이 순서',
      p: `안내문은 <b>날짜·시간·대상·요금·신청 방법·주의 사항</b>이 반복해서 출제된다. 선지가 우리말이면 선지 순서와 안내문 항목 순서가 대체로 같으므로 위에서부터 차례로 대조한다. under, at least, no later than 같은 <span class="hl">경계 표현</span>에 주의한다.`,
      table: { head: ['항목', '자주 나오는 표현', '함정'], rows: [
        ['대상', 'aged 12–15, under 7', 'under 7은 7세 미포함'],
        ['요금', 'free with a paying adult, 10% off', '조건이 붙은 할인·무료'],
        ['신청', 'by July 10, at least 2 days in advance', '마감일·현장 접수 여부'],
        ['주의', 'non-transferable, rain or shine', '양도 불가, 날씨와 관계없이 진행'],
      ] },
      ex: [['Admission is free for children under 7.', '7세 미만 어린이는 무료이다. (7세는 유료)'],
           ['Tours will run rain or shine.', '투어는 날씨와 관계없이 진행된다.']] },
    { h: '내용 일치(인물·소재) 전략',
      p: `인물 전기문이나 소재 설명문은 <b>선지 순서 = 지문 순서</b>인 경우가 많다. 선지를 먼저 읽고 핵심어(연도, 지명, 직업)를 표시한 뒤 지문에서 그 부분만 정밀하게 대조한다. 지문의 표현을 살짝 바꾼 선지(학문 분야, 숫자, 긍정/부정)가 오답으로 나온다.`,
      table: { head: ['바뀌는 부분', '지문', '오답 선지 예'], rows: [
        ['분야', 'studied engineering', '건축학을 공부했다'],
        ['시기·순서', 'worked on bridges before architecture', '곧바로 건물 설계를 시작했다'],
        ['긍정/부정', 'refused invitations', '제안을 수락했다'],
        ['주체', 'farmers, not officials, applied it', '정부 기관이 채택했다'],
      ] },
      warn: '배경지식으로 판단하지 말 것. 반드시 지문에 쓰인 내용만 근거로 한다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `Sales rose from 20 to 60 units. 이 변화를 바르게 표현한 것은?`, o: [`Sales doubled.`, `Sales tripled.`, `Sales fell by 40 units.`, `Sales remained steady.`], a: 1,
      why: `60 ÷ 20 = 3이므로 세 배가 되었다(tripled). 40 units 증가했지 감소한 것이 아니다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `A rate of 30% rose to 45%. 이 변화를 바르게 표현한 것은?`, o: [`It rose by 15 percent.`, `It rose by 45 percentage points.`, `It rose by 15 percentage points.`, `It halved.`], a: 2,
      why: `% 수치끼리의 차이(45 − 30 = 15)는 percentage points로 말한다. 15 percent 증가라면 30%의 1.15배인 34.5%가 된다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `Admission is free for children under 7. 7세 어린이의 입장 조건은?`, o: [`유료`, `무료`, `입장 불가`, `보호자 동반 시 무료`], a: 0,
      why: `under 7은 7세 미만(6세 이하)을 뜻하므로 7세는 무료 대상이 아니다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `표의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<table><tr><th>Country</th><th>2010</th><th>2020</th></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>12</td><td>18</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>25</td><td>20</td></tr><tr><td>C</td><td>8</td><td>16</td></tr></table>`, o: [`B's figure increased.`, `A had the highest figure in 2020.`, `The figures of all countries rose.`, `C's figure doubled from 2010 to 2020.`], a: 3,
      why: `C는 8에서 16으로 두 배가 되었다. B는 감소했고, 2020년 최고는 B(20)이므로 나머지는 틀리다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Library Book Sale</b><br>• Saturday, May 9, 10 a.m.–3 p.m.<br>• All books are $1 each.<br>• Cash only, please.<br>• All proceeds go to the children's reading program.</div>`, o: [`토요일에 열린다.`, `모든 책은 1달러이다.`, `카드 결제가 가능하다.`, `수익금은 어린이 독서 프로그램에 쓰인다.`], a: 2,
      why: `Cash only(현금만 가능)이므로 카드 결제는 안 된다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<br>Elena Varga was born in 1902 in a small Hungarian village. At 19 she moved to Vienna to study botany, and she later spent ten years collecting plants in South America.`, o: [`그녀는 빈에서 태어났다.`, `19세에 빈으로 이주했다.`, `동물학을 공부했다.`, `남아메리카에서 20년을 보냈다.`], a: 1,
      why: `At 19 she moved to Vienna가 근거이다. 헝가리 마을에서 태어났고, 식물학(botany)을 공부했으며, 남아메리카에서는 10년을 보냈다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 도표의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Main Reading Format of Survey Respondents (%)</b><table><tr><th>Format</th><th>2018</th><th>2023</th></tr><tr><td>Printed books</td><td>58</td><td>41</td></tr><tr><td>E-books</td><td>27</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Audiobooks</td><td>9</td><td>21</td></tr><tr><td>Other</td><td>6</td><td>8</td></tr></table>The table above shows the share of survey respondents by their main reading format in 2018 and 2023. ① In both years, printed books were the most common reading format. ② The share of respondents who mainly used printed books dropped by 17 percentage points between 2018 and 2023. ③ The share of audiobook users more than doubled over the period. ④ In 2023, the share of e-book users was more than twice that of audiobook users.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `2023년 전자책 30%, 오디오북 21%이므로 두 배가 넘지 않는다. ②는 58 − 41 = 17, ③은 9 → 21로 두 배 이상이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Riverside Science Camp 2026</b><br>• Dates: July 20–24 (Monday–Friday)<br>• Time: 9:00 a.m.–3:00 p.m.<br>• Who: Students aged 12–15<br>• Fee: $150 (lunch included) / 10% discount for siblings<br>• Activities: robotics, chemistry experiments, and star-gazing (optional evening session on Thursday, 7–9 p.m.)<br>• Registration: Online only, by July 10. No refunds after July 15.<br>• Please bring a water bottle and comfortable shoes.</div>`, o: [`등록은 현장에서만 가능하다.`, `캠프는 5일 동안 진행된다.`, `참가비에 점심 식사가 포함되어 있다.`, `목요일 저녁 활동은 선택 사항이다.`], a: 0,
      why: `Registration은 Online only(온라인만)이다. 월~금 5일, lunch included, optional evening session은 모두 일치한다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `Tomas Arlen에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage">Tomas Arlen (1891–1964) was a Norwegian architect known for buildings that blended modern materials with traditional forms. The son of a carpenter, he spent his childhood helping in his father's workshop, where he developed a deep respect for wood. After studying engineering in Berlin, he returned home in 1918 and worked for several years designing bridges before turning to architecture. His first major project, a public library completed in 1931, combined a steel frame with a steeply sloping wooden roof inspired by rural farmhouses. Critics at first dismissed the design as old-fashioned, but it soon became a model for younger architects. In his later years, Arlen devoted himself to teaching and wrote a widely read book on the use of local materials. He refused several invitations to work abroad, insisting that architecture should grow from its own landscape.</div>`, o: [`베를린에서 건축학을 공부한 후 곧바로 건물 설계를 시작했다.`, `첫 주요 작품은 1931년에 완공된 미술관이었다.`, `목수인 아버지의 작업장에서 어린 시절을 보냈다.`, `해외에서 일해 달라는 제안을 여러 차례 수락했다.`], a: 2,
      why: `The son of a carpenter … helping in his father's workshop이 근거이다. 베를린에서는 공학을 공부했고 다리 설계를 먼저 했으며, 첫 작품은 도서관, 해외 제안은 거절했다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 도표의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Average Daily Screen Time by Age Group (hours)</b><table><tr><th>Age group</th><th>2020</th><th>2024</th></tr><tr><td>13–18</td><td>5.2</td><td>6.1</td></tr><tr><td>19–29</td><td>4.8</td><td>5.0</td></tr><tr><td>30–49</td><td>3.5</td><td>4.2</td></tr><tr><td>50+</td><td>2.4</td><td>3.6</td></tr></table>The table above shows the average daily screen time of four age groups in 2020 and 2024. ① In both years, the 13–18 group spent the most time on screens. ② The 30–49 group showed the largest increase in screen time between 2020 and 2024. ③ The screen time of the 19–29 group increased by less than half an hour. ④ In 2024, the screen time of the 50+ group was 1.5 times its 2020 level.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 1,
      why: `증가량은 13–18이 0.9, 19–29가 0.2, 30–49가 0.7, 50+가 1.2시간이므로 가장 크게 늘어난 것은 50+이다. ④는 3.6 ÷ 2.4 = 1.5로 맞다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage"><b>City History Museum: Lantern Night Tour</b><br>• Dates: Every Friday in October<br>• Time: 7:30 p.m.–9:00 p.m. (Doors open at 7:00 p.m.)<br>• Tickets: Adults $12 / Students $8 (ID required) / Children under 10 free with a paying adult<br>• Each tour is limited to 20 people. Reservations must be made at least 2 days in advance.<br>• Lanterns will be provided. Flash photography is not permitted inside the galleries.<br>• Tours will run rain or shine.</div>`, o: [`투어는 10월 매주 토요일에 진행된다.`, `관람객은 각자 손전등을 가져와야 한다.`, `비가 오면 투어가 취소된다.`, `학생은 신분증이 있으면 8달러에 참가할 수 있다.`], a: 3,
      why: `Students $8 (ID required)가 근거이다. 매주 금요일이며, 랜턴은 제공되고, rain or shine이므로 비가 와도 진행된다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `axolotl에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage">The axolotl, a salamander native to a few lakes near Mexico City, has long fascinated biologists because of its extraordinary ability to regenerate. Unlike most animals, it can regrow not only lost limbs but also parts of its heart, spinal cord, and even brain, without forming scar tissue. Another unusual trait is that it typically remains in its juvenile form throughout life, keeping its feathery external gills and living entirely in water. In the wild, however, the axolotl is critically endangered. Urban expansion, water pollution, and the introduction of non-native fish have greatly reduced its habitat. Ironically, the species thrives in laboratories and home aquariums around the world. Researchers hope that understanding its regenerative abilities may one day help develop treatments for human injuries.</div>`, o: [`멕시코시티 인근 몇몇 호수가 원산지이다.`, `성체가 되면 아가미를 잃고 육지에서 생활한다.`, `팔다리뿐 아니라 뇌 일부도 재생할 수 있다.`, `야생에서는 심각한 멸종 위기에 처해 있다.`], a: 1,
      why: `평생 어린 형태를 유지하며 겉아가미를 지닌 채 물속에서만 산다(living entirely in water)고 했다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 도표의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Share of Electricity Generation by Source in Country X (%)</b><table><tr><th>Source</th><th>2015</th><th>2025</th></tr><tr><td>Coal</td><td>45</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Natural gas</td><td>25</td><td>28</td></tr><tr><td>Nuclear</td><td>18</td><td>17</td></tr><tr><td>Renewables</td><td>12</td><td>25</td></tr></table>The table above shows the share of electricity generation by source in Country X in 2015 and 2025. ① In 2025, the combined share of coal and natural gas was less than half of total generation. ② The share of renewables more than doubled over the decade, surpassing that of nuclear in 2025. ③ Natural gas was the second largest source of electricity in both years. ④ The share of nuclear power changed by less than 2 percentage points between the two years.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 0,
      why: `2025년 석탄 30 + 천연가스 28 = 58%로 절반을 넘는다. ②는 12 → 25(두 배 초과)이고 원자력 17보다 크며, ③은 두 해 모두 두 번째, ④는 1%p 변화이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 안내문의 내용과 일치하는 것은?<div class="passage"><b>Greenfield Community Pool: Summer Passes</b><br>• Single visit: Adults $6 / Youth (6–17) $4 / Under 6 free<br>• Season pass: Adults $90 / Youth $60<br>• Family season pass (2 adults + up to 3 youths): $220<br>• Season pass holders receive 20% off swimming lessons.<br>• The pool is closed every Monday for cleaning.<br>• Passes are non-transferable.</div>`, o: [`5세 아이는 1회 입장 시 4달러를 낸다.`, `수영장은 매주 일요일에 청소를 위해 문을 닫는다.`, `성인 2명과 청소년 3명은 가족 시즌권으로 개별 시즌권보다 140달러를 아낀다.`, `시즌권은 다른 사람에게 양도할 수 있다.`], a: 2,
      why: `개별 시즌권은 90 × 2 + 60 × 3 = 360달러, 가족 시즌권은 220달러이므로 140달러를 아낀다. 6세 미만은 무료, 휴무는 월요일, 양도 불가이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `Ingrid Solberg에 관한 다음 글의 내용과 일치하지 <b>않는</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Ingrid Solberg (1908–1987) was a Swedish chemist whose work on soil helped transform agriculture in northern Europe. Although her parents hoped she would become a teacher, she enrolled in chemistry at university at the age of seventeen, one of only three women in her class. Her early research focused on industrial dyes, but a summer spent on her uncle's farm convinced her that the chemistry of soil was a more urgent problem. In 1936 she published a study showing that small amounts of certain minerals could double the yield of crops grown in acidic soil. The findings were initially ignored by government agencies, and it was farmers' cooperatives, not officials, who first applied her methods. Solberg never held a permanent university post; instead, she ran a small independent laboratory funded largely by those cooperatives. She was elected to the national academy of sciences in 1961.</div>`, o: [`부모는 그녀가 교사가 되기를 바랐다.`, `초기 연구는 산업용 염료에 관한 것이었다.`, `영구적인 대학 교수직을 맡지 않았다.`, `그녀의 연구는 처음에 정부 기관에 의해 적극적으로 채택되었다.`], a: 3,
      why: `처음에는 정부 기관이 무시했고(initially ignored), 농민 협동조합이 먼저 적용했다. 나머지는 지문과 일치한다.` },
  ],
});

ENG.add('h3', '장문 독해(1지문 2문항)', {
  sub: '긴 지문을 한 번 읽고 제목·빈칸·낱말을 함께 푼다',
  kw: ['장문 독해', '1지문 2문항', '제목', '낱말 쓰임', '빈칸', '시간 관리'],
  sections: [
    { h: '장문 유형과 읽는 순서',
      p: `수능 장문은 <b>1지문 2문항(41~42번)</b>과 <b>1지문 3문항(43~45번)</b>으로 나온다. 1지문 2문항은 주로 <span class="hl">제목(또는 요지)과 문맥상 낱말 쓰임</span>을 함께 묻는다. 지문이 길기 때문에 문항을 먼저 확인하고, 한 번 읽으면서 두 문항을 동시에 해결하는 것이 핵심이다.`,
      table: { head: ['단계', '할 일', '체크 포인트'], rows: [
        ['1', '두 문항의 유형 확인', '제목 + 낱말 / 빈칸 + 낱말 등'],
        ['2', '첫 단락에서 화제와 통념 파악', 'However 뒤 필자의 관점'],
        ['3', '읽으면서 밑줄 낱말 즉시 판단', '앞뒤 인과·대조 방향'],
        ['4', '마지막 단락에서 주제 확정 후 제목 선택', '결론 문장 재진술'],
      ], cap: '한 번 읽고 두 문항을 끝낸다' },
      ex: [['Comfort, it turns out, can be the enemy of lasting knowledge.', '알고 보니 편안함은 오래가는 지식의 적일 수 있다.'],
           ['The lesson for organizations is clear.', '조직에 주는 교훈은 분명하다. (결론 신호)']] },
    { h: '장문 제목·빈칸 전략',
      p: `장문의 제목은 <b>글 전체</b>를 덮어야 한다. 단락마다 예시가 달라지므로 한 단락의 예시만 담은 제목은 오답이다. 빈칸은 대개 마지막 단락의 결론에 있으며, 앞 단락들의 공통 내용을 재진술한 선지가 정답이다.`,
      table: { head: ['출제 포인트', '정답의 특징', '오답의 특징'], rows: [
        ['제목', '모든 단락을 포괄, 핵심어 + 관점', '한 단락의 예시, 통념 그대로'],
        ['빈칸(결론)', '앞 단락의 원리를 일반화', '지문 단어 재활용, 반대 방향'],
        ['요지', '필자의 주장·교훈', '사실 진술만 담은 선지'],
      ] },
      ex: [['Struggle That Strengthens', '강하게 만드는 고투 (제목형 표현)'],
           ['The Hidden Wisdom of Forgetting', '망각에 숨은 지혜']] },
    { h: '장문 낱말 쓰임과 시간 관리',
      p: `장문의 낱말 문제는 단문 어휘 문제와 원리가 같지만, 근거가 <b>다른 단락</b>에 있을 수 있다. 밑줄 낱말을 보면 그 단락의 주장과 방향이 맞는지 바로 판단하고, 확신이 없으면 표시해 두었다가 글을 다 읽은 뒤 다시 본다.`,
      table: { head: ['상황', '대처', '시간 목표'], rows: [
        ['밑줄이 반의어로 바뀐 경우', '반대말 대입 테스트', '읽으면서 즉시'],
        ['근거가 뒤 단락에 있는 경우', '표시 후 마지막에 재확인', '30초 이내'],
        ['1지문 2문항 전체', '한 번 읽고 두 문항 해결', '약 3~4분'],
      ] },
      tip: '마지막 문단의 결론 문장을 기준으로 앞의 밑줄을 다시 점검하면 실수가 줄어든다.' },
  ],
  bank: [
    { lv: 1, q: `수능 장문 1지문 2문항(41~42번)에서 주로 함께 묻는 것은?`, o: [`어법과 도표`, `안내문과 내용 일치`, `제목과 문맥상 낱말 쓰임`, `듣기와 대화`], a: 2,
      why: `1지문 2문항은 주로 제목(또는 요지)과 문맥상 적절하지 않은 낱말을 묻는다. 1지문 3문항은 순서·지칭·내용 일치를 묻는다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `밑줄 친 she가 가리키는 대상은?<br>When Mina met her grandmother, <u>she</u> smiled because she had baked bread for her granddaughter.`, o: [`Mina`, `Mina's grandmother`, `a baker`, `Mina's friend`], a: 1,
      why: `손녀(granddaughter)를 위해 빵을 구운 사람은 할머니이므로 미소 지은 she도 할머니이다.` },
    { lv: 1, q: `문맥상 어색한 밑줄 낱말을 고칠 말로 알맞은 것은?<br>Because the bridge was <u>sturdy</u>, it collapsed under a light load.`, o: [`weak`, `strong`, `new`, `famous`], a: 0,
      why: `가벼운 하중에 무너졌으므로 다리는 '튼튼한(sturdy)' 것이 아니라 '약한(weak)' 것이어야 한다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">A small town once relied on a single factory. When it closed, many expected the town to fade away. Instead, residents turned the empty building into studios and markets, and within a decade the town had become known for its art. The loss that seemed to end the town's story had become the start of a new one.</div>`, o: [`From Closure to Creative Rebirth`, `Why Factories Must Never Close`, `The Decline of Small-Town Art`, `How to Build a Successful Factory`], a: 0,
      why: `공장 폐쇄라는 위기가 예술 도시로 거듭나는 출발점이 되었다는 내용이다. ③은 반대 방향이다.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">Early explorers relied on the stars because they were <u>①&nbsp;constant</u> guides: their positions changed in <u>②&nbsp;predictable</u> ways. Sailors who could read them were able to cross oceans with <u>③&nbsp;confidence</u>. Cloudy nights, however, made navigation much <u>④&nbsp;easier</u>.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `however로 방향이 바뀌므로 구름 낀 밤에는 별을 볼 수 없어 항해가 '더 어려워졌다(harder)'.` },
    { lv: 2, q: `장문 1지문 2문항을 푸는 방법으로 가장 효율적인 것은?`, o: [`지문을 끝까지 다 읽은 뒤에야 문항을 확인한다.`, `선지부터 모두 해석한 뒤 지문을 읽는다.`, `문항 유형을 먼저 확인하고, 읽으면서 밑줄·빈칸 주변의 단서를 표시한다.`, `밑줄 친 낱말이 있는 문장만 골라 읽고 답을 고른다.`], a: 2,
      why: `문항을 먼저 확인하면 한 번 읽으면서 두 문항을 함께 해결할 수 있다. 밑줄 문장만 읽으면 다른 단락의 근거를 놓친다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When people learn a new skill, they often hope to avoid mistakes as much as possible. Teachers and coaches, too, sometimes design lessons so that students rarely fail, believing that success builds confidence. Research on learning, however, suggests that a certain amount of difficulty can make knowledge last longer. In one line of studies, students who were tested on material before they had studied it, and who therefore answered most questions incorrectly, later remembered the material better than students who had simply read it. The initial failure seemed to prepare the mind to receive the correct information, making it more noticeable and meaningful when it finally arrived.<br>Psychologists use the term "desirable difficulties" to describe such challenges. Spacing study sessions over several days instead of cramming, mixing different types of problems instead of practicing one type at a time, and trying to recall information rather than rereading it all feel harder in the moment. Learners often judge these methods to be less effective because progress seems slower. Yet tests given weeks later consistently show that they produce deeper and more durable learning.<br>The key word, however, is "desirable." Difficulties help only when learners have enough background knowledge to eventually overcome them. A task that is far beyond a student's ability produces frustration rather than learning. The goal, therefore, is not to make learning as hard as possible but to find the level of challenge that forces the mind to work without overwhelming it. Comfort, it turns out, can be the enemy of lasting knowledge.</div>`, o: [`Why Mistakes Should Always Be Avoided`, `Cramming: The Fastest Route to Mastery`, `Struggle That Strengthens: The Value of Productive Difficulty`, `How Teachers Can Make Lessons Comfortable`], a: 2,
      why: `적절한 어려움이 더 오래가는 학습을 만든다는 글 전체 내용을 담은 제목이다. ①④는 필자가 반박하는 통념이고, ②의 벼락치기는 덜 효과적인 방법으로 제시되었다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Every day, people make choices without realizing that the options have been arranged for them. A cafeteria that places fruit at eye level and desserts at the back will sell more fruit, even though no one is forbidden from buying cake. Economists and psychologists refer to this arrangement as choice architecture, and one of its most powerful tools is the default option: what happens if a person does nothing.<br>Consider retirement savings. In many companies, employees used to be required to sign up actively for a savings plan, and a large proportion never did, despite knowing that saving was wise. When some companies changed the rule so that employees were enrolled automatically unless they chose to leave the plan, participation rose dramatically. The options were identical in both cases; only the starting point had changed. Similar effects have been observed in organ donation, where countries that treat citizens as donors unless they opt out have far higher donation rates than those requiring people to opt in.<br>Why are defaults so influential? Part of the reason is inertia: changing a setting requires effort, however small. Another part is that people often interpret the default as a recommendation, assuming that whoever designed the system chose the sensible option. Because of this, those who design choices carry a responsibility they may not recognize. There is no neutral way to present options; some arrangement must come first. In other words, <span class="blank"></span>, and the only question is whether it is done thoughtfully.</div>`, o: [`every system of choices inevitably influences behavior`, `people always choose the healthiest option`, `defaults can be removed from most decisions`, `most people ignore the options presented to them`], a: 0,
      why: `'선택지를 중립적으로 제시하는 방법은 없다'를 재진술한 것이므로 모든 선택 구조는 불가피하게 행동에 영향을 준다. ③은 '어떤 배열이든 먼저 와야 한다'와 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 3, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">When a honeybee colony grows too large, thousands of bees leave the hive with their queen and gather in a cluster on a nearby branch. They now face a life-or-death decision: they must choose a new home, and a poor choice, such as a cavity that is too small, too damp, or too exposed, can doom the colony during the coming winter. Remarkably, the decision is made without any leader. The queen plays no role in choosing.<br>Instead, a few hundred scout bees fly out to inspect possible sites. When a scout returns, it performs a dance whose duration reflects the site's quality: the better the site, the <u>①&nbsp;longer</u> the dance. Other scouts that watch the dance may fly out to inspect the same site and, if they are impressed, dance for it as well. Support for good sites therefore grows, while support for poorer ones <u>②&nbsp;fades</u>, partly because scouts dance less enthusiastically each time they return.<br>The process ends when enough scouts gather at a single site. Once that threshold is reached, the scouts return and signal the swarm to fly. Studies show that the colony chooses the best available site in most cases, an impressive result for a group whose members each have only <u>③&nbsp;limited</u> information.<br>Researchers see lessons here for human groups. The bees' success depends on independent evaluation, open competition among ideas, and a rule that prevents the group from deciding too <u>④&nbsp;slowly</u>, before enough evidence has been gathered. Committees that rush to agreement, or that defer to a single dominant voice, often perform worse than a swarm of insects.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 3,
      why: `'충분한 증거가 모이기 전에' 결정하는 것을 막는 규칙이므로 너무 '성급하게(quickly)' 결정하지 않게 하는 것이다. 뒤의 '합의를 서두르는 위원회'와도 연결된다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 글의 요지로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We often imagine creativity as the ability to produce something entirely new, a flash of inspiration that arrives from nowhere. The history of art and science, however, tells a different story. Most innovations are combinations of existing ideas, brought together in ways that no one had tried before. The printing press combined the screw press used for making wine with techniques for casting metal. Many celebrated musical styles emerged when musicians from different traditions met in port cities and borrowed each other's rhythms and instruments.<br>If creativity depends on combination, then the raw material matters enormously. A person who knows only one field has fewer pieces to combine than someone whose interests range widely. This may explain why many influential thinkers had unusual hobbies or careers that crossed boundaries. A biologist who also studies architecture may notice structural patterns that specialists in either field would miss.<br>This view also changes how we might think about originality. If every creation borrows from what came before, then the fear of copying should not prevent people from studying the work of others closely. Imitation, practiced openly and with understanding, is often the first stage of invention. Young painters have long learned by copying masterpieces, not to reproduce them forever, but to absorb techniques they would later transform.<br>None of this makes creativity easy. Combining ideas well requires judgment about which pieces fit together and persistence through many failed attempts. But it suggests that creativity is less a mysterious gift than a habit of collecting widely and connecting boldly.</div>`, o: [`진정한 창의성은 다른 사람의 작품을 전혀 참고하지 않을 때 생긴다.`, `창의성은 다양한 기존 아이디어를 폭넓게 모으고 결합하는 데서 나온다.`, `한 분야를 깊이 파고드는 전문가만이 혁신을 이룬다.`, `모방은 예술가의 독창성을 영구적으로 해친다.`], a: 1,
      why: `마지막 문장 '창의성은 신비한 재능이라기보다 폭넓게 모으고 대담하게 연결하는 습관'이 요지이다. ①④는 모방이 발명의 첫 단계라는 내용과 반대이다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">Imagine being given a coffee mug and then being asked how much money you would accept to sell it. Now imagine that, instead, you were offered the chance to buy the same mug. Logically, the two prices should be about the same: the mug is identical, and so is your taste for coffee. Yet experiments of this kind consistently find that owners demand roughly twice as much to give up an object as non-owners are willing to pay to obtain it.<br>This gap, known as the endowment effect, reflects a broader tendency called loss aversion. Losses tend to feel more painful than equivalent gains feel pleasant. Once an object becomes ours, parting with it is experienced as a loss, while acquiring it was only ever a potential gain. The effect appears even when ownership lasts only a few minutes, suggesting that it is not based on emotional attachment built up over time.<br>The consequences extend well beyond mugs. Homeowners often set asking prices far above market value and wait months for buyers who never appear. Investors hold on to falling stocks rather than accept a loss, and employees resist changes in working conditions that would leave them better off overall, because they focus on what they would give up. Even governments find it hard to remove existing programs, whatever their costs.<br>Understanding this bias can lead to better decisions. One useful question is whether we would choose to acquire something today if we did not already have it. If the answer is no, then our reluctance to let it go may be driven less by its value than by <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`, o: [`its actual price in the market`, `the opinions of other people`, `the simple fact that it is already ours`, `a careful calculation of future gains`], a: 2,
      why: `소유하는 순간 내놓는 것이 손실로 느껴진다는 보유 효과가 글의 핵심이므로, 놓지 못하는 이유는 가치가 아니라 '이미 내 것이라는 사실'이다. ①은 '가치보다는'과 같은 내용이라 대조가 성립하지 않는다.` },
    { lv: 4, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">For most of human history, the night sky was one of the most familiar sights in the world. People used the stars to tell time, to navigate, and to tell stories about their origins. Today, however, a large proportion of the world's population lives under skies so bright that the Milky Way is no longer visible. Artificial light, scattered by particles in the atmosphere, creates a glow that <u>①&nbsp;reveals</u> all but the brightest stars.<br>The loss is not merely aesthetic. Many animals depend on natural patterns of light and darkness. Newly hatched sea turtles, for instance, find the ocean by moving toward the brighter horizon over the water; near illuminated beaches, they crawl inland instead, often to their deaths. Migrating birds that navigate by the stars can become <u>②&nbsp;disoriented</u> by brightly lit buildings, circling them until they are exhausted. Even humans are affected: exposure to light at night can <u>③&nbsp;disrupt</u> the body's internal clock and interfere with sleep.<br>Unlike many forms of pollution, light pollution is relatively easy to reduce. Shielding streetlights so that they point downward, using warmer colors, and switching off unnecessary lights late at night can dramatically reduce the glow without compromising safety. Some towns that have adopted such measures have even become destinations for tourists hoping to see a truly dark sky.<br>What makes the problem persistent is not technical difficulty but lack of attention. Because the change happened gradually, few people notice what has been lost. Restoring the night sky, therefore, begins with <u>④&nbsp;recognizing</u> that it is worth seeing.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 0,
      why: `은하수가 보이지 않을 만큼 밝은 하늘이므로 인공 불빛은 가장 밝은 별을 제외한 모든 별을 '가린다(hides)'. reveals는 반대 방향이다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 글의 제목으로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">We tend to regard forgetting as a failure of memory, an unfortunate leak in a system designed to store information. Recent research in neuroscience suggests a different view: forgetting may be an active process that the brain performs deliberately, and one that is essential for thinking well.<br>Consider what would happen if we remembered everything. Every face we glimpsed on a crowded street, every number we dialed, every trivial conversation would compete for attention whenever we tried to recall something important. Rare individuals with extraordinarily complete autobiographical memories often describe this not as a gift but as a burden, reporting that they are constantly pulled into the past. A mind that cannot let go of details may also struggle to see the general patterns that those details share.<br>Forgetting, in this sense, works like editing. By discarding specifics that are rarely used, the brain makes room for generalizations that apply across many situations. A child who forgets the exact appearance of each dog she has met but retains the general concept of "dog" is better prepared to recognize a new one. Studies in animals have even identified mechanisms that actively weaken certain memories, suggesting that the brain invests energy in removing information rather than merely losing it.<br>This does not mean that all forgetting is beneficial; diseases that destroy memory cause real suffering. But it suggests that the goal of memory is not to preserve the past perfectly. It is to keep what is useful for the future, and that requires the ability to let much of the past go.</div>`, o: [`How to Remember Everything You Learn`, `Memory Diseases and Their Treatments`, `Why Children Learn Faster Than Adults`, `The Hidden Wisdom of Forgetting`], a: 3,
      why: `망각이 일반화를 가능하게 하는 능동적이고 유익한 과정이라는 글 전체의 내용을 담는다. ①은 필자가 부담이라고 본 '모든 것을 기억하기'이고, ②③은 일부 소재에 그친다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `다음 빈칸에 들어갈 말로 가장 적절한 것은?<div class="passage">When engineers develop a more efficient engine or light bulb, it seems obvious that energy consumption will fall. If a car can travel twice as far on the same amount of fuel, its owner should need only half as much fuel. In the nineteenth century, however, an economist studying coal noticed something puzzling: as steam engines became more efficient, Britain's total consumption of coal did not decline but increased sharply.<br>The explanation lies in how people respond to lower costs. Greater efficiency reduces the cost of using a resource for a given purpose. That reduction makes the activity more attractive, so people do more of it, and it opens up new uses that were previously too expensive. Cheaper lighting led not to the same number of lit rooms using less energy but to brightly lit streets, shop windows, and stadiums. More efficient cars encourage longer commutes and larger vehicles.<br>Economists debate how large this rebound effect is. In some cases, only a small part of the expected savings is lost; in others, consumption actually rises above its original level. The size depends on how strongly demand responds to price and whether the savings are spent on other energy-intensive activities.<br>For policymakers, the lesson is not that efficiency is useless. Efficient technologies make it possible to achieve more with less, which is valuable in itself. But if the goal is to reduce total consumption, efficiency alone may not be enough. Without additional measures such as pricing or limits, <span class="blank"></span>.</div>`, o: [`technology will eventually stop improving`, `gains in efficiency may be partly or entirely consumed by increased use`, `people will refuse to buy efficient products`, `energy prices will fall to zero`], a: 1,
      why: `효율이 높아지면 사용이 늘어 절약분 일부가 사라지거나(rebound) 오히려 소비가 늘 수 있다는 글이므로, 추가 조치가 없으면 효율 향상이 사용 증가로 상쇄될 수 있다.` },
    { lv: 5, q: `밑줄 친 낱말 중 문맥상 쓰임이 적절하지 <b>않은</b> 것은?<div class="passage">In the early twentieth century, a scientist at a country fair examined the guesses of hundreds of visitors who had tried to estimate the weight of an ox. Individually, the guesses varied widely, and many were far from correct. Their average, however, was astonishingly close to the true weight. This kind of observation has since been repeated in many settings, from estimating the number of beans in a jar to forecasting election results.<br>Why should a crowd be <u>①&nbsp;wiser</u> than most of its members? The answer lies in how errors behave. Each person's estimate contains some knowledge and some error. If the errors are random, some people guess too high and others too low, and when the estimates are combined, the errors tend to <u>②&nbsp;accumulate</u>, leaving the shared knowledge behind.<br>This mechanism, however, depends on an important condition: the guesses must be <u>③&nbsp;independent</u>. When people hear one another's opinions before forming their own, their errors become correlated. A confident early speaker can pull the whole group in the same direction, and the crowd loses the diversity that made it accurate. Experiments have shown that even modest social influence can make group estimates less accurate while making participants feel more confident.<br>The lesson for organizations is clear. Meetings in which senior members speak first may produce a sense of agreement, but they often <u>④&nbsp;suppress</u> the very diversity of judgment that good decisions require. Asking people to write down their views before discussion begins is a simple way to protect the independence that gives a group its collective intelligence.</div>`, o: [`①`, `②`, `③`, `④`], a: 1,
      why: `너무 높은 추정과 너무 낮은 추정이 합쳐지면 오차가 서로 '상쇄되어(cancel out)' 공유된 지식만 남는다. accumulate(쌓이다)는 반대 방향이다.` },
  ],
});
